// Generated by the keyword plan build. Server-only (blog and tool pages). Do not edit by hand.
export const BLOG_BODIES = {"off-road-vs-street-legal-australia":{"hook":"In Australia, a pedal-assist e-bike with a 250W motor that meets the EPAC standard can be used on public roads. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only.","intro":["In Australia, a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W standard can be used on public roads and paths, subject to state and territory rules. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes with more powerful motors are for off-road and private property use only.","That one line settles most buying decisions, but the details matter: what counts as pedal assist, who checks it, and what changes from state to state. This guide explains the difference in plain language and points you to the regulators who write the rules."],"sections":[{"h":"Road-legal e-bikes: the 250W pedal-assist rule","p":["The e-bikes allowed on public roads are treated as bicycles. In general they have a motor rated at no more than 250W continuous power, the motor assists only while you pedal, and the assistance cuts out once you reach about 25 km/h. Bikes built to the European EN 15194 standard (often called EPAC) are designed to meet these limits.","A small throttle is sometimes allowed for walk-assist at very low speed, but a bike whose throttle can drive the motor at higher speeds is treated differently. Check the rule in your state before assuming a throttle bike is road legal.","If you want a bike for commuting, start with the [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/) or [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) categories, where pedal-assist models are the norm."]},{"h":"Electric dirt bikes and e-motos: off-road and private property only","p":["Electric dirt bikes, mini bikes and most e-motos have motors well above 250W and are not built to road standards. They are sold for off-road and private property use only. They are not road legal, and they cannot simply be registered for the road.","Torque E-Bikes lists these bikes with that wording on purpose, and we will not describe a high-power bike as street legal. If you are comparing [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/), plan where you will ride them first: a private property, a licensed track or an approved off-road area."]},{"h":"State and territory differences","p":["Road rules are set by each state and territory, so details such as helmet rules, where you can ride on footpaths and how fast a power-assisted bike may be assisted can differ. Always check the current rule with the regulator for the state where you will ride."],"ul":["New South Wales: Transport for NSW (transport.nsw.gov.au)","Victoria: VicRoads (vicroads.vic.gov.au)","Queensland: Department of Transport and Main Roads (tmr.qld.gov.au)","Western Australia: Department of Transport (transport.wa.gov.au)","South Australia: Department for Infrastructure and Transport (dit.sa.gov.au)","Tasmania, the ACT and the Northern Territory: the state or territory road authority"]},{"h":"Age, licence and helmet requirements","p":["On public roads an e-bike that meets the pedal-assist rules is treated like a bicycle, and riders must wear an approved helmet. For dirt bikes used on private land, the land owner and your state may set their own rules on age, supervision and licensing. Do not rely on a general guide for numbers that vary by state: read the regulator page and the land owner’s conditions.","For riding gear that suits each type of riding, see our guide to [helmets and protective gear for e-bikes](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"What the rules mean for conversion kits","p":["A conversion kit can turn an ordinary bicycle into an e-bike. A pedal-assist kit that stays within the 250W limits can be a road-legal e-bike, but a higher-powered kit makes the bike an off-road machine. Fitting a powerful kit to a bicycle does not make it a registered motorcycle.","Our [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) explain how to match a kit to where you plan to ride."]},{"h":"Common misunderstandings about e-bike rules","p":["A few myths cause most of the confusion. The first is that any bike with pedals is a bicycle. A bike with pedals but a powerful throttle motor is still treated by its power and how it is driven, not by whether it has pedals. The second is that a “street legal” label from a seller settles the question. The label does not decide it: the rules do, and they are set by your state or territory.","The third is that a conversion kit is exempt. Fitting a kit changes the bike, and if the result exceeds the limits for a power-assisted bicycle, the bike is no longer one. If you are unsure, ask the regulator for your state before you ride on a public road."]},{"h":"Riding on bike paths, footpaths and trails","p":["Where a road-legal e-bike may ride on paths and footpaths varies by state and by council, and shared paths often have their own speed limits and signs. Many national parks, mountain bike parks and trail networks also set their own rules for e-bikes, and some restrict them to certain trails.","Before riding somewhere new, check with the land manager. For off-road bikes, riding on public land is usually not permitted at all, so plan to ride on private property or at a venue that allows electric bikes."]},{"h":"Insurance, liability and landowners","p":["Insurance cover for e-bikes and off-road bikes differs between insurers. Some home and contents policies cover e-bikes, others exclude motorised vehicles, and compulsory third-party insurance does not apply to bikes that are not registered for the road. Ask your insurer in writing what is covered, particularly for a high-powered bike or a conversion.","If you ride on someone else’s land, get their permission and understand their conditions. A landowner may require supervision, protective gear or a signed waiver, and you remain responsible for your own safety and for other people on the property."]},{"h":"How to check a rule yourself","ul":["Search the regulator’s website for your state with the words “power-assisted bicycle” or “e-bike”.","Check the date on the page, because rules change.","Look for the motor power limit, the speed at which assistance cuts out and any throttle conditions.","If the page does not answer your question, phone or email the regulator and keep a copy of the reply."],"p":["This guide is general information, not legal advice. Torque E-Bikes does not set the rules, and the regulator for your state has the final say."]},{"h":"Power, speed and what “pedal assist” really means","p":["The headline number in most discussions is 250W, but the rule is about more than watts. A road-legal power-assisted bicycle is one where the motor helps only while you pedal, the motor’s continuous rated power stays within the limit, and the assistance stops at a set speed (commonly 25 km/h). Peak power on a spec sheet can be higher than continuous power, so look for the continuous rating and any compliance statement from the maker.","A bike can have pedals and a motor and still fall outside the definition. If the motor can drive the bike without pedalling at speeds above walking pace, or if its rated power is above the limit, regulators treat it differently. That is why a fat-tyre bike with a 750W motor and a thumb throttle is sold as off-road or private-property equipment, however many pedals it has."]},{"h":"How regulators describe e-bikes and e-motos","p":["Each state road authority publishes its own wording, but the shape is the same. A power-assisted pedal cycle that meets the definition is a bicycle, so bicycle road rules apply. Anything more powerful is a motor vehicle, and a motor vehicle used on a public road needs to meet vehicle standards, be registered and be ridden by a licensed rider. Off-road electric motorbikes are generally not built to those standards, which is why they are limited to private property.","The practical rule is simple: if a seller cannot show you that a bike meets the pedal-assist definition for your state, do not assume it can be used on public roads. Ask for the compliance statement in writing, and check it against the regulator’s page."]},{"h":"Buying from overseas and “grey” imports","p":["Bikes ordered from overseas sellers are often built to the rules of another country. A bike sold as an “EPAC” in Europe is designed around the 250W and 25 km/h limits, but a bike from a market with higher limits may not comply in Australia even if it looks identical. Imported bikes can also arrive without the compliance information, warranty or parts support that you would expect locally.","Before you import or buy a parallel-import model, check the motor rating, the assist cut-off speed, whether there is a throttle and how it behaves, and who will honour the warranty. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply to goods sold to Australian consumers, but chasing an overseas seller is harder than dealing with a local one."]},{"h":"Common scenarios","ul":["**Commuting on a 250W pedal-assist bike:** generally treated as a bicycle. Wear an approved helmet, use lights at night and follow the rules for shared paths in your state.","**Riding a 1,000W electric dirt bike to the shops:** not permitted. It is an off-road machine, and riding it on a public road risks fines and loss of licence.","**Letting a teenager ride a high-powered e-bike on private land:** the road rules do not apply on private land, but safety, supervision and the landowner’s conditions do. Read our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) before you decide.","**Fitting a bigger motor or removing a speed limiter:** it can change the legal status of the bike. Check with your state road authority before you modify a road-legal bike.","**Hiring or lending an e-bike:** the person riding is responsible for riding it lawfully, so check the bike meets the rules where they will ride."]},{"h":"Penalties, safety and sensible habits","p":["Penalties for riding an unregistered high-powered bike on a public road can include fines, demerit points and the bike being impounded, and insurance may not respond if you have a crash. Even where a bike is allowed on the road, e-bikes are heavier and faster than they feel, so ride predictably, give way on shared paths and keep your speed down around pedestrians.","A few habits make the biggest difference: wear a helmet every time, keep your lights charged, service your brakes before you need them, and keep the compliance information for your bike with your receipt in case anyone asks. If you are ever unsure about a rule, ask the regulator rather than relying on a forum post."]},{"h":"A state-by-state checking routine","p":["Because rules sit with each state and territory, the most reliable way to stay legal is a short routine you repeat when you buy a bike, move state or modify the bike. First, find the road authority’s page on power-assisted bicycles or e-bikes and note the date. Second, write down the motor power limit, the speed at which assistance must stop, any throttle conditions and the helmet and lighting rules. Third, compare that list with your bike’s specification and compliance statement. Fourth, keep a copy of the page and the specification with your purchase records.","If anything does not match, ask the seller to explain why, or ask the regulator by email. A written answer from the regulator is worth far more than a forum post when a police officer or insurer asks questions."]},{"h":"Hire bikes, workplaces and shared schemes","p":["Hire and share schemes carry their own conditions, such as age limits, speed caps and restricted areas. If you ride a hired e-bike you are still responsible for riding it lawfully. At work, check whether your employer allows e-bikes inside buildings or in bike storage, because lithium battery safety rules are becoming common, and some buildings restrict charging."]},{"h":"Teenagers, young riders and supervision","p":["Age rules for e-bikes and for off-road bikes differ, and they depend on where the bike is ridden. On a public road, a young rider on a road-legal e-bike follows bicycle rules and helmet requirements. On private land, there may be no legal age limit for an off-road bike, but you carry the responsibility. Choose a bike sized for the child, supervise every ride, insist on full protective gear and set clear boundaries. Our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and [electric balance bike guide](/blog/electric-balance-bikes/) cover this in more detail."]},{"h":"A three-question buying checklist","ul":["Where will I ride? Roads and bike paths point to a 250W pedal-assist e-bike. Private land and trails point to an off-road bike.","How much power do I need? More power is not better if it puts the bike outside the rules you need to follow.","Who is the rider? Age, height and experience matter for dirt bikes, so check the maker’s guidance and read our [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for young riders."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are e-bikes legal in Australia?","a":"Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W EPAC standard can be used on public roads and paths, subject to state rules. Electric dirt bikes and higher-powered e-bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Always check your state or territory road authority before you ride."},{"q":"Do I need a licence or registration for an e-bike?","a":"A compliant 250W pedal-assist e-bike is generally treated like a bicycle, but rules differ by state, so confirm with your road authority. Electric dirt bikes are not registrable for road use in Australia and are limited to private property and off-road riding."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell road-legal e-bikes?","a":"Yes. The commuter, folding and cargo categories include pedal-assist e-bikes. Each product page states its motor power, and anything above 250W is marked off-road only. Message the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure which model suits where you ride."},{"q":"What is the difference between a road-legal e-bike and an electric dirt bike?","a":"A road-legal e-bike adds a limited 250W motor to a pedalled bicycle. An electric dirt bike is a motorbike-style machine with far more power and no pedal-assist limit, so it is restricted to private land and off-road use. They suit different riders and different places."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes that are road legal and those that are off-road only?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes that are road legal and those that are off-road only in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric bikes and the law","supporting":["e bike legislation","australian states are proposing stricter e-bike laws and age limits","legal e bikes","ebike rules","are surrons legal in australia"]},"words":1950},"best-electric-bikes-in-australia":{"hook":"The best electric bike depends on where you ride. Commuter e-bikes suit daily trips, e-mountain bikes suit trails, folding e-bikes suit small spaces and cargo e-bikes suit loads.","intro":["The best electric bike in Australia is the one that fits how and where you ride. A bike that is perfect for a flat daily commute is wrong for steep trails, and a trail bike is wasted on a short ride to the station.","Instead of a single ranking, this guide shows how to narrow the choice in five steps, then points you to the right category so you can compare real models side by side in Australian dollars."],"sections":[{"h":"Start with the type of riding","p":["Match the bike to the journey. Commuting and errands suit pedal-assist city bikes with mudguards, lights and a rack. Trail riding suits an e-mountain bike. Sand, gravel and rough tracks suit a fat-tyre bike. Small flats and mixed transport suit a folding bike, and loads or children suit a cargo bike."],"ul":["City and commuting: [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)","Trails and hills: [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/)","Sand, gravel and rough tracks: [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/)","Small spaces and mixed transport: [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/)","Loads and family: [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/)"]},{"h":"Check the motor and the rules","p":["In Australia a pedal-assist e-bike with a 250W motor can use public roads, subject to state rules. Bikes with more powerful motors are for off-road and private property use only. Read the product page, and see our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) if you are unsure."]},{"h":"Battery size and real-world range","p":["Battery capacity is usually quoted in watt-hours (Wh). A larger battery gives more range but adds weight and cost. The range in a brochure is a best case: hills, headwinds, heavy riders, cold weather and high assist levels all shorten it.","Choose a battery with some spare capacity for your longest regular trip. Our [e-bike battery guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) explains how to look after it so it lasts."]},{"h":"Brakes, frame and components","p":["E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so brakes matter. Look for hydraulic disc brakes on hilly or fast bikes, and check that the frame size suits your height. Reliable components and spare parts matter more than a long feature list."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before you buy any e-bike","ul":["What is the motor’s rated power, and does the bike meet the pedal-assist limits if I want to ride it on roads?","How big is the battery, and what real-world range do riders report for my weight and terrain?","Who services the bike, and are spare parts such as batteries, chargers and brake pads easy to get?","What does the warranty cover, for how long, and who handles a claim?","How heavy is it, and can I lift it, store it and carry it if I need to?"]},{"h":"Price bands and what to expect","p":["E-bike prices cover a wide range, and a higher price usually buys a better motor and battery, better brakes, a stronger frame and a longer warranty. Lower-priced bikes can be good value for short, flat trips, but check the brakes, battery and warranty with extra care.","Rather than compare across types, compare bikes of the same type in the same price band. A commuter e-bike and an e-mountain bike at the same price will have very different strengths. The [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models, and each category page shows its price range in Australian dollars."]},{"h":"Weight, storage and charging at home","p":["E-bikes are far heavier than normal bikes. If you live in an apartment, need to carry the bike upstairs or put it in a car, weight and a removable battery matter. Plan where the bike will live and where you will charge it, because a battery should be charged in a ventilated spot away from anything flammable.","If space is tight, a folding bike may suit better: see the [folding e-bike guide](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Test ride and fit","p":["A bike that is the wrong size will never feel right. Check the maker’s size chart, and if you can, sit on the bike or compare it with one you already ride. If you are buying online, ask the seller about returns and fitment advice before ordering, and message the team if you want a second opinion on size."]},{"h":"Commuter e-bikes: the everyday choice","p":["If most of your riding is to work, the shops or the station, a commuter e-bike gives the best return. Look for a comfortable upright position, mudguards, a rear rack, built-in lights and tyres that handle wet roads. A step-through frame makes starting and stopping easier, and a removable battery makes charging at work or in an apartment simple.","Commuters should put reliability ahead of headline features. Check that brake pads, tyres and chains are standard sizes you can buy locally, and that the seller can supply spare parts. See the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) for a checklist, and browse [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Electric mountain bikes: trails and hills","p":["An e-MTB suits riders who want to climb more and ride further on trails. Suspension, strong brakes and a durable frame matter more than a long feature list, and a mid-drive motor with a torque sensor usually feels the most natural on technical climbs. Check where you plan to ride, because some trail networks restrict e-bikes.","Our [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) covers hardtail versus full suspension, motors and battery size, and you can compare models in the [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Folding, cargo and fat-tyre bikes: special jobs","ul":["**Folding e-bikes** suit small flats and mixed transport. Check folded size and weight: [folding e-bike guide](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/).","**Cargo e-bikes and trikes** carry shopping, tools or children. Check load ratings and brakes: [cargo guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) and [trike guide](/blog/electric-trikes-for-adults/).","**Fat-tyre e-bikes** handle sand, gravel and rough tracks. They are heavier and can be less efficient on hard roads: [fat-tyre guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Understanding battery size with a simple example","p":["Battery capacity in watt-hours is the voltage multiplied by the amp-hours. A 36V battery rated at 10Ah holds 360Wh, and a 48V battery at 14Ah holds 672Wh. Many riders see consumption somewhere in the region of 10 to 25 Wh per kilometre depending on assist level, hills, rider weight and wind, so a 500Wh battery might cover anywhere from about 20 to 50 km. Treat that as a rough guide, not a promise.","If your daily round trip is 30 km with hills, a 500Wh battery used at medium assist will be working hard, and a larger pack or a second battery gives you a safety margin. If your trip is 8 km on flat ground, a smaller battery is fine and saves weight and money."]},{"h":"Warranty, servicing and what “good value” means","p":["A cheaper bike that cannot be serviced is poor value. Ask who services the bike, how long parts take to arrive and what the warranty covers. Batteries and motors are the costly parts, so find out whether they are covered and for how long, and whether the seller stocks replacements. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply on top of any warranty.","Good value usually means a bike that suits your riding, uses common wearable parts, has clear specifications and comes with someone who will answer your questions. If you want help comparing two bikes, message the team with your budget and typical trip."]},{"h":"Three example shopping lists","ul":["**The first-time commuter:** a step-through commuter e-bike, mid-size battery, mudguards, rack, lights, a D-lock, an approved helmet and a repair kit.","**The weekend trail rider:** a hardtail e-MTB with hydraulic brakes, a larger battery, a trail helmet, gloves, a pump and spare tube, and a rack for the car.","**The family:** a longtail cargo e-bike with child seats, a strong stand, good lights, two locks and a rain cover."],"p":["Use these as a starting point and adjust for your budget and where you ride. A good bike with the right accessories will serve you better than an expensive bike you cannot use comfortably."]},{"h":"Mistakes first-time buyers make","ul":["Buying on price alone and ignoring brakes and warranty.","Choosing the wrong frame size.","Forgetting about storage, charging and weight.","Assuming any e-bike can be ridden on any road or trail.","Skipping a helmet, lights and a lock."]},{"h":"Set a budget and compare like for like","p":["Prices span a wide range depending on motor, battery and frame. If you are watching costs, the [budget electric bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models, and the [all e-bikes](/electric-bikes/) page lets you browse by type.","Compare two or three bikes of the same type, in the same price band, on motor, battery, brakes, weight and warranty. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help narrowing the list."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I look for in an electric bike?","a":"Start with where you will ride, then check motor power, battery capacity, frame type and brakes. Compare the stated range with your usual trip length, and confirm whether the model is road legal or off-road only. Weight and warranty support also matter for daily use."},{"q":"How far can an electric bike go on one charge?","a":"Range depends on battery size, rider weight, terrain and assist level, so treat any claim as a guide. Check the manufacturer figure on the product page and allow a margin for hills and headwinds. A larger battery or lower assist setting stretches the distance."},{"q":"Which brands does Torque E-Bikes stock?","a":"The range covers more than 100 brands across e-bikes, electric dirt bikes, parts and gear. Browse the brand pages to see each maker’s current models and prices, or filter the shop by category if you already know the type of bike you want."},{"q":"Is it better to buy a cheap e-bike or a more expensive one?","a":"A lower price can suit short, flat trips, but check the battery, brakes and warranty before deciding. Higher-priced bikes usually add stronger components and longer range. Compare two or three models in the same category rather than judging by price alone."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list the electric bikes this guide compares?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists the electric bikes this guide compares in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric bikes guide","supporting":["electric bikes in guide","how to choose electric bikes in","electric bikes in explained","electric bikes in for beginners","electric bikes in tips"]},"words":1313},"e-bike-brands-in-australia":{"hook":"E-bike brands differ mainly by bike type, price band and parts quality. This guide groups the brands listed at Torque E-Bikes by what each one makes.","intro":["People searching for e-bike brands in Australia often start with household names such as Norco, Avanti, Orbea and Focus, then discover that many electric bikes are sold under smaller brands. A brand name tells you who stands behind the bike, but it does not tell you how good the bike is.","This guide explains what to compare between brands, how local support affects ownership, and where to see the brands Torque E-Bikes lists. We do not stock every brand mentioned here, and we only describe the brands we list."],"sections":[{"h":"Why a brand name is only part of the answer","p":["Established bicycle makers often add electric models to a range they already sell through bike shops. Newer e-bike brands may sell online and ship direct. Neither is automatically better. The questions that matter are the same: who sells it, who services it, and where do the parts come from?"]},{"h":"What to compare between brands","ul":["Motor and battery: who makes them, how common are they, and can you buy a replacement?","Warranty: how long, what it covers and who handles a claim in Australia.","Parts and servicing: are spare parts listed, and can a local bike shop work on the bike?","Frame and components: brakes, gears, tyres and suspension suited to how you ride.","Price: compare bikes of the same type and spec, not different categories."]},{"h":"Imported brands versus locally supported brands","p":["An imported brand is not worse by default, but local support changes the ownership experience. Check who handles warranty, how long parts take to arrive and whether the seller can answer technical questions. Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply whichever brand you buy."]},{"h":"Brands you can browse at Torque E-Bikes","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes, e-bikes, parts and gear from many brands. You can browse them all on the [brands page](/brands/), or open a brand to see its full range with prices in Australian dollars.","If you are comparing dirt bikes, start with the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and the [Talaria guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/). For fat-tyre bikes, see the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"A short checklist for judging any brand","ul":["How long has the brand been selling, and does it publish clear specifications?","Does it list warranty terms and a way to contact support?","Are spare parts, such as batteries and chargers, available separately?","Do independent owners describe the same strengths and problems over time?","Does the seller explain the rules for riding in Australia honestly?"]},{"h":"Why the same bike can appear under different names","p":["Many e-bikes share frames, motors and batteries from the same factories, then are sold under different brand names. That is not a problem in itself, but it means a brand name does not guarantee unique engineering. Compare the specification and the support, not the logo."]},{"h":"Where to start for each type of rider","ul":["Commuting: see the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) and the [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/) category.","Trails: see the [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/).","Off-road bikes: see the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/).","Sand and gravel: see the [fat-tyre guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"How to read a spec sheet without being fooled","p":["Spec sheets are written to sell, so read them with a few filters in mind. Motor power is often quoted as peak power rather than continuous power. Battery capacity should be given in watt-hours: if only volts or amp-hours are shown, multiply them to compare like with like. Range figures are almost always best case, and weights may exclude the battery.","Look for the details that tell you how the bike is built: brake brand and type, motor and sensor type, frame material, tyre width, rider weight limit and the warranty terms. A brand that is open about these is usually easier to deal with after the sale."]},{"h":"Service, warranty and what happens when something breaks","p":["Every brand has a good day. What separates brands is what happens on a bad one. Before you buy, find out whether you would send the bike to a local workshop or ship it, how long parts take, and whether the seller will help you diagnose a fault. Batteries and controllers are the parts most likely to need attention, so check how they are covered.","Australian Consumer Law gives you rights if a product is faulty, and those rights apply whichever brand you choose. In practice a seller that responds quickly and stocks spare parts is worth more than a famous logo."]},{"h":"Ten questions to ask before buying from any brand","ul":["Who sells and services the bike in Australia?","What is the warranty and who handles claims?","What are the motor and battery brands?","How big is the battery in watt-hours?","Are spare parts available separately?","What is the bike’s weight and rider limit?","Does the bike meet the pedal-assist definition for road use?","What is included in the box?","What do independent owners say after a year of use?","How is a lithium battery shipped to my address?"]},{"h":"Brands we do not stock","p":["Searches for Norco, Avanti, Orbea, Specialized, Focus and similar names are common, but Torque E-Bikes does not list those brands. If one of them is your first choice, we would rather you buy it from an authorised dealer than guess. If you want an alternative, tell us your budget and riding and we can suggest something from the range."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I compare e-bike brands?","a":"Compare the same type of bike across brands, then check motor, battery capacity, brake type, frame material and warranty support in Australia. Spare-part availability matters as much as the headline specification. Price should be judged against those points, not on its own."},{"q":"Does the brand matter more than the specs?","a":"Specs describe what the bike can do, while the brand tells you how well it is supported. A well-supported brand makes warranty claims, batteries and spare parts easier to get in Australia. Look at both, and favour clear specifications over marketing claims."},{"q":"Can I see all brands in one place?","a":"Yes. The brand pages list every product from that maker with current prices, and the shop can be filtered by category. Start with the brand hub for the make you are interested in, then compare it with another brand in the same category."},{"q":"Is an imported brand worse than an Australian-supported one?","a":"Not necessarily, but local support changes the ownership experience. Check who handles warranty, how parts are supplied and whether a battery replacement is available. Brands with a local distributor are usually easier to service than direct imports. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list each brand mentioned in this guide?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists each brand mentioned in this guide in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e-bike brands guide","supporting":["norco","ebox","focus bikes","orbea","malvern star bike"]},"words":793},"e-bike-conversion-kits-explained":{"hook":"An e-bike conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to an existing bicycle. Kits above 250W are for off-road and private property use only in Australia.","intro":["A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle so it becomes an e-bike. It is often cheaper than buying a new e-bike if you already own a bike you like, but only if the bike suits the kit and you are happy to handle installation.","This guide covers what is in a kit, the main kit types, what to check before you buy and how Australian rules apply. When you are ready, our [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) can point you to a suitable kit."],"sections":[{"h":"What a conversion kit includes","ul":["A motor: a hub motor built into a wheel, or a mid-drive motor that drives the chain.","A battery and charger, and sometimes a battery mount.","A controller, display and wiring harness.","Throttle or pedal-assist sensor, brake cut-off levers and fixings."],"p":["Check exactly what is in the box. Some kits omit the battery, and extras such as torque arms or a rack may be sold separately."]},{"h":"Hub motor or mid-drive?","p":["A hub kit replaces the front or rear wheel and is the simplest to fit. A mid-drive kit replaces the crankset and uses the bike’s gears, which suits hills and gives a natural feel, but it is more work to install and needs a compatible bottom bracket."]},{"h":"Will it fit your bike?","p":["Check the frame material and strength, the dropout width, the wheel size, the brake type and the room for a battery. High-powered motors put extra force on the dropouts, so heavy-duty kits need torque arms and a strong frame."],"ul":["Dropout width and axle size match the motor.","Wheel size and brake type (rim or disc) match the wheel.","There is space for the battery and wiring.","The frame can take the extra weight."]},{"h":"The 250W rule and off-road kits","p":["A pedal-assist kit that keeps the bike within the 250W limits can result in a road-legal e-bike. More powerful kits turn the bike into an off-road machine for private property use. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before buying."]},{"h":"Batteries, brakes and safety","p":["The battery is the most important safety item in a kit. Buy a quality pack, use the supplied charger and follow our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/). Upgrade the brakes if you add power: see the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/).","Browse current kits and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category, and the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category."]},{"h":"Installation: what to expect","p":["Fitting a hub-motor kit usually means removing your wheel, fitting the motor wheel and torque arms, mounting the battery, running the wiring and setting up the controller, display and brake cut-off sensors. A confident home mechanic can often do this in a day. A mid-drive kit takes longer, because the crankset and bottom bracket come off.","Take your time with the wiring: connectors must be fully seated, wires secured away from moving parts and the battery mounted so it cannot move. If you are not confident with the electrical side, ask a bike shop or a qualified person to check the work before you ride."]},{"h":"How much does a conversion really cost?","p":["The kit price is only part of the total. Add a battery if it is not included, any brake or wheel upgrades, torque arms, a rack for the battery, tools and your time or a workshop’s labour. Compare that total with a ready-made e-bike of the same type before you decide, and remember that a ready-made bike is designed as a system."]},{"h":"Common mistakes","ul":["Choosing a kit that is too powerful for the frame.","Ignoring torque arms on high-powered motors.","Using the wrong battery connector or an unprotected battery.","Leaving the old brakes in place on a faster, heavier bike.","Assuming a converted bike is road legal without checking the rules."]},{"h":"Looking after a converted bike","p":["Check bolts, torque arms and wiring regularly in the first weeks of use, because vibration loosens things. Keep the chain clean and aligned (see the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)), look after the battery, and replace brake pads and tyres early rather than late."]},{"h":"Choosing power, voltage and battery for your build","p":["Voltage and power are related but not the same. A 36V or 48V kit is common for road-style conversions that stay near the 250W limit, while higher-voltage systems are used in off-road builds. Higher voltage lets a motor deliver more power at lower current, but it also means more serious wiring, a stronger controller and more care with safety.","Match the battery to the kit: the voltage must match the controller, the connector must suit the wiring and the pack must be able to supply the current the motor will draw. A battery that cannot supply enough current will cut out under load, and one that is oversized for the controller can damage it."]},{"h":"A worked example: converting a commuter bike","p":["Say you own a hybrid bike with rim brakes and a 700c rear wheel, and you want an e-bike for a 12 km commute with a few hills. A rear hub-motor kit built for a 700c wheel with a 250W motor, a 36V battery of around 400 to 500Wh and a basic display would fit the job. The steps are to check the rear dropout width and axle size, order a kit with the right wheel size, fit the wheel and torque arm, mount the battery and wiring, set up the brake cut-offs and test the system before you ride.","The same bike could take a mid-drive kit if you want to use the gears on hills, but you would need a compatible bottom bracket and more installation time. For most commuters the hub kit is the simpler choice."]},{"h":"Checklist before you buy a kit","ul":["Frame material and condition: look for cracks and check the dropouts are strong.","Rear dropout width (for example 135 mm on many mountain and hybrid bikes) and axle type.","Wheel size and rim width for the motor wheel.","Brake type: rim or disc, and the room for a disc rotor on a hub motor.","Gear type: cassette or freewheel, and the number of speeds.","Battery location: frame mount, rack mount or bottle-cage style.","Display, throttle or pedal-assist sensor needs.","Warranty, return policy and spare parts for the motor and controller."]},{"h":"Safety, testing and servicing after the build","p":["Test the system with the wheel off the ground first, then ride slowly in an empty area. Check that the brake cut-offs work, that the motor does not surge and that all connectors and bolts stay tight. After the first few rides, re-check the torque arms, axle nuts and wiring, because vibration loosens things.","Keep records of what you fitted, the battery model and the date. If you sell or lend the bike later, the next owner should know it has been converted and what it is rated for. See also our [DIY conversion planning guide](/blog/diy-2000w-conversion-guide/) for off-road builds."]},{"h":"Where to buy parts and who can help","p":["Browse kits, motors and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category and batteries in the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category. If you are unsure whether a kit suits your bike, send the team your bike’s make, model, wheel size and brake type and we will tell you what we think will fit before you order."]},{"h":"Quick answers from people thinking about a conversion","ul":["**Can I convert a carbon frame?** Many makers advise against hub motor conversions on carbon frames because of clamping and torque loads. Check with the frame maker.","**Will a conversion void my bike’s warranty?** It can. Check the frame warranty before you modify the bike.","**How long does it take?** A confident home mechanic can fit a hub kit in a few hours to a day, and a mid-drive kit takes longer.","**Can I take the kit off later?** Usually yes, if you keep the original wheel and parts, but check for drilled holes or modified dropouts.","**Do I need special tools?** Common bike tools, a torque wrench and basic electrical tools such as crimpers and a multimeter."]},{"h":"Total cost and time planning","p":["Add up the kit, battery, brake upgrades, tools, accessories and your time. Compare that total with a ready-made e-bike that includes a warranty and support. If the numbers are close, the ready-made bike is usually the lower-risk choice. If you love your existing bike or want a custom build, a conversion can be very satisfying."]},{"h":"Kit or ready-made e-bike?","p":["A kit can cost less if you already own a suitable bike, but you also pay for a battery, tools and time. A ready-made e-bike is simpler and its parts are designed to work together. If you are unsure, compare the full cost of each before you decide."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What does an e-bike conversion kit include?","a":"A kit normally includes a motor, battery, controller, throttle or pedal sensor, display and wiring. Some kits add torque arms and a charger. Check the contents list carefully, because brakes, cranks and mounting hardware are often sold separately. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can any bicycle be converted to electric?","a":"Many bicycles can be converted, but the frame, wheel size, dropout spacing and brake type must suit the motor you choose. High-power kits need a strong frame and good brakes. Ask the team on WhatsApp to check compatibility before you order."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell conversion kits?","a":"Yes. The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists conversion kits, motors, torque arms and drivetrain parts. Each listing shows the motor power, and kits above 250W are marked as off-road and private property use only. Open the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a conversion kit cheaper than buying a new e-bike?","a":"A kit can cost less if you already own a suitable bike, but the total depends on the battery, installation time and any parts that need upgrading. Compare the full cost of the kit and upgrades against a ready-made e-bike in the same category."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list conversion kits?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists conversion kits in the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"conversion kit","supporting":["cycle conversion kit","conversion kit with bike","conversion kit guide","how to choose conversion kit","conversion kit explained"]},"words":1346},"best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia":{"hook":"The best electric dirt bike depends on rider size, terrain and budget. Lightweight models suit beginners and trails, while higher-power bikes suit experienced riders on tracks.","intro":["An electric dirt bike is a battery-powered off-road motorbike with no fuel, no clutch and a quiet motor. In Australia, high-powered models are for off-road and private property use only, so the first question is where you will ride.","Rather than name a single winner, this guide shows how to choose between models by rider, power and battery, then points you to the range so you can compare real bikes."],"sections":[{"h":"Decide where you will ride","p":["Private property, a licensed track or an approved off-road area are the places to ride a dirt bike. Check the land owner’s rules and your state’s requirements first. Dirt bikes are not road legal, and we do not sell them as street-legal machines."]},{"h":"Match the bike to the rider","p":["Seat height, weight and power delivery matter more than headline numbers. A lighter, lower-powered bike is easier to learn on and is usually the better first purchase for an adult beginner. Young riders need a model sized to their height with a power limiter: see the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Motor power and battery capacity","p":["More power gives stronger acceleration and more speed, but it is harder to control and drains the battery faster. Battery capacity decides how long you can ride. Compare the motor rating, voltage and battery size in the product specification, and keep in mind that real riding time depends on terrain, rider weight and how hard you ride."]},{"h":"Popular brands and models","p":["Sur-Ron and Talaria are the names most riders ask about. Read the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and the [Talaria guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/) for how the ranges are laid out, then browse every [electric dirt bike](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) we list."]},{"h":"Choosing a bike for a beginner adult","p":["If you have never ridden an off-road motorbike, choose a lighter bike with a low-power mode and learn the throttle gently. Electric power is instant, so a bike that feels tame in a low mode can surprise you in a high one. Take a first ride in an open area away from obstacles.","Taller or heavier riders should check the seat height, suspension and rider weight limit. A bike that is too small or too weak is uncomfortable and wears out faster."]},{"h":"Weight, handling and suspension","p":["Weight affects how easy the bike is to pick up, load and manoeuvre. Suspension travel and quality affect comfort and control on rough ground. A heavier bike with better suspension can be easier to ride fast, while a lighter bike is easier to learn on. Choose for how you ride, not for the highest number on the specification."]},{"h":"Charging, ride time and spare batteries","p":["Ride time depends on battery size and how hard you ride. For long days, consider a second battery or a larger pack, and plan where you will charge. Follow the maker’s charging instructions and the safety advice in the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Running costs and maintenance","p":["Electric dirt bikes have no engine oil, filters or spark plugs, so routine servicing is simpler. You still need to look after brakes, tyres, chain, sprockets and the battery. Budget for wear items such as tyres and brake pads, and for protective gear."]},{"h":"Understanding riding modes and power delivery","p":["Most electric dirt bikes let you choose between modes that limit power and sometimes top speed. A low mode softens the throttle and is ideal for learning, a middle mode suits general trail riding, and a high mode gives full power for experienced riders. Take the time to learn each one in an open space before you ride on tighter trails.","Because electric torque arrives instantly, the way the throttle is mapped matters. A bike with smooth, progressive delivery is easier to control than one that jumps on the first twist. If you can, ask riders who own the model how it feels in the lowest mode."]},{"h":"Where to ride legally and responsibly","p":["In Australia, high-powered electric dirt bikes are for private property and approved off-road areas only. Public parks, footpaths, roads and most bushland are off limits. Look for licensed motocross tracks, private land where you have permission and venues that accept electric bikes, and always check the operator’s rules and any age or licence requirements first.","Quiet bikes are easier on neighbours, but they still need courtesy: keep to agreed hours, stay inside boundaries and respect other users. Our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) explains why these bikes cannot be used on public roads."]},{"h":"A short pre-ride checklist","ul":["Check tyre pressure and look for damage.","Test both brakes and make sure the levers feel firm.","Check the chain tension and sprocket bolts.","Check the battery is secure and charged.","Wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour."]},{"h":"Buying checklist for an electric dirt bike","ul":["Rider height, weight and experience match the bike.","Motor power and riding modes suit the riding area.","Battery capacity and charger are appropriate, with the option of a spare.","Brakes, suspension and tyres suit the terrain.","Parts, warranty and support are available in Australia.","Delivery arrangements for a lithium battery are clear.","Protective gear is included in the budget."]},{"h":"Adult, youth and mini bikes","p":["Adult bikes are larger, with more power and suspension. Youth bikes are sized for teenagers and smaller adults, with lower power. Mini bikes and balance bikes suit children. Choose the size that suits the rider now, not the one they might grow into. See the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [Yozma IN10 guide](/blog/yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/)."]},{"h":"Frequently asked questions from new riders","ul":["**Do I need a licence?** Rules depend on where you ride. On private land there is usually no road licence, but check the venue’s and landowner’s requirements.","**Can I ride it on the road?** No. Electric dirt bikes are off-road only.","**How long does charging take?** It depends on the battery and charger, so check the product page.","**Can I ride in the rain?** Check the maker’s guidance, and avoid submerging the bike or pressure-washing electrical parts.","**What is the best first bike?** A lighter bike with riding modes that suits your size is usually the best start."]},{"h":"Maintenance schedule","ul":["After every ride: wipe the bike, check tyres and brakes.","Every month: lubricate and check chain tension, check bolts.","Every few months: check brake pads, rotors, bearings and suspension.","Every year: service suspension and replace worn drivetrain parts."]},{"h":"Gear, charging and care","p":["Wear a certified helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour every time you ride: see our [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/). Follow the maker’s charging instructions and our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/), and keep the drivetrain clean and tight with the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric dirt bikes good for beginners?","a":"Electric dirt bikes have no clutch or gearbox and deliver smooth power, which many beginners find easier to learn on. Choose a lightweight model, start in a low power mode if available and wear full protective gear. Ride only on private property or approved off-road areas."},{"q":"How long does an electric dirt bike battery last?","a":"Ride time depends on battery capacity, terrain, rider weight and how hard the bike is ridden. Treat the manufacturer figure as a guide and expect less on steep or sandy ground. A second battery or a fast charger extends your riding day."},{"q":"Which electric dirt bike brands does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The range includes Sur-Ron, Talaria, Segway, KTM, Husqvarna and other makers, plus kids’ electric dirt bikes. Open the electric dirt bikes category and filter by brand to see current models and prices. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an electric dirt bike better than a petrol one?","a":"Electric bikes are quieter, need less maintenance and have no fuel or clutch, but ride time is limited by the battery. Petrol bikes refuel quickly and suit very long days. The better choice depends on where you ride and how long you ride for."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric dirt bikes for adults?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes for adults in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric dirt bikes guide","supporting":["electric dirt bikes in guide","how to choose electric dirt bikes in","electric dirt bikes in explained","electric dirt bikes in for beginners","electric dirt bikes in tips"]},"words":1010},"sur-ron-models-explained":{"hook":"Sur-Ron makes several electric off-road bike models, including the Light Bee, Ultra Bee, Storm Bee and Hyper Bee. They differ in power, battery and intended use.","intro":["Sur-Ron makes some of the best-known electric off-road bikes, and its model names can be confusing. The Light Bee is the lightweight bike most people picture, the Storm Bee is a larger, heavier-duty platform, and the Ultra Bee sits at the high-power end.","This guide explains how the range is organised and what to compare. Specifications change between model years, so use it to build a shortlist, then confirm the details on the product page and with the manufacturer."],"sections":[{"h":"The Sur-Ron family at a glance","ul":["**Light Bee (including Light Bee X and Light Bee S):** the lightweight bikes, popular for trails and mixed riding.","**Storm Bee:** a larger platform built for harder off-road use.","**Ultra Bee:** the high-power model, for experienced riders.","**Hyper Bee and others:** newer variants that appear from time to time. Check the current listing."]},{"h":"Light Bee X and Light Bee S","p":["The Light Bee X and Light Bee S are the usual starting points. They are light for an electric off-road bike, which makes them approachable, but they are still powerful bikes that need protective gear and a safe place to ride. See current listings in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category."]},{"h":"Storm Bee and Ultra Bee","p":["Larger, more powerful models give stronger acceleration and more capability on rough terrain, but they ask more of the rider. If you are new to dirt bikes, start with something lighter and move up when you are confident."]},{"h":"What to compare between models","ul":["Motor power and how it is delivered (riding modes).","Battery voltage and capacity, which decide how long you can ride.","Weight, seat height and suspension travel.","Rider size and experience level.","Spare parts and upgrade availability: see [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/)."]},{"h":"Choosing between the Light Bee and the Storm Bee","p":["The Light Bee suits riders who want a light, easy-to-handle bike for trails and mixed riding. The Storm Bee is a heavier-duty platform for riders who want more capability on rough terrain and are comfortable with a larger bike. If you are not sure, the lighter bike is usually the safer starting point, and you can always move up later."]},{"h":"Upgrades and spares","p":["Sur-Ron bikes have a strong aftermarket for wheels, suspension, brakes, swingarms and charging parts. Always match each part to your exact model and year, because components are not always interchangeable between versions. See the [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) category and ask the team to confirm fitment."]},{"h":"Model years and what changes","p":["Specifications and parts change between model years, so a part for an earlier Light Bee may not suit a newer one. When you compare bikes, compare the same model year, and check what is included, such as the charger and any tools."]},{"h":"A simple way to shortlist a Sur-Ron","ul":["List the riders: height, weight and experience.","List the terrain: tight trails, open tracks, hills or sand.","Pick the lightest bike that handles that terrain, because weight makes the bike easier to learn and recover.","Check which riding modes you get and whether they can be limited.","Check parts, charger and warranty before comparing price."]},{"h":"Charging, batteries and range planning","p":["Charging time and range vary by battery size and charger. Plan rides around the battery you have, carry a spare if you want a long day, and charge in a ventilated spot away from anything flammable. Do not leave the battery charging unattended, and store it partly charged if the bike will sit for weeks. Our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) has the details."]},{"h":"Real-world questions Sur-Ron buyers ask","ul":["**Is it too much bike for a beginner?** Lighter models in a low riding mode are manageable, but power is instant, so learn in an open area with full gear.","**Can a taller or heavier rider use a Light Bee?** Check the rider limits and think about suspension setup, because very tall riders can find the bike small.","**How long does a ride last?** It depends on the battery, terrain and mode, so plan around a margin and consider a spare battery.","**What do I need to buy with the bike?** Protective gear, a lock and tools, and a safe place to charge."]},{"h":"Keeping a Sur-Ron reliable","p":["Wear items such as tyres, brake pads, chains and sprockets need regular checks, and the aftermarket offers many parts. Keep a log of what you replace, tighten bolts often because off-road riding shakes them loose, and keep the battery connectors clean and dry. See [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) and the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Safety, supervision and riding area","p":["A Sur-Ron is a capable machine. Learn the controls in an open area, wear full protective gear and ride only where you have permission. Do not ride alone in remote areas, tell someone where you are going, and carry a phone and first aid kit. Check brakes, tyres and bolts before every ride."]},{"h":"Rules and gear","p":["Sur-Ron bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia. They are not road legal, and we do not describe them as street legal. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and wear full protective gear: see the [gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/).","Curious about speed and range? Read [how fast a Sur-Ron goes](/blog/how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Sur-Ron Light Bee and Ultra Bee?","a":"The Ultra Bee is the larger, more powerful of the two, while the Light Bee is lighter and aimed at trail and everyday riding. Compare the manufacturer’s published motor, battery and weight figures for the exact model year before choosing."},{"q":"Are Sur-Ron bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"No. Sur-Ron bikes are off-road electric motorbikes and are not road legal or registrable in Australia. Use them only on private property or approved off-road areas, and check local rules for where riding is permitted. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes stock Sur-Ron bikes and parts?","a":"Yes. The Sur-Ron brand page lists the bikes currently available plus spares and accessories. Browse the Sur-Ron spares category for swingarms, wheels, charging parts and more, or ask the team to confirm fitment for your model. Open the sur-ron category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Which Sur-Ron model is best for a first-time buyer?","a":"A lighter, lower-powered model is usually easier to learn on, but the right choice depends on rider size and terrain. Compare the models in the same price band and keep budget for protective gear and a spare battery. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the sur-ron range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Sur-Ron models?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Sur-Ron models in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"surron lightbee","supporting":["surron hyperbee","surron ultra bee hp","e bikes ultra bee","surron lightbee x","surron lightbee guide"]},"words":783},"how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go":{"hook":"A Sur-Ron’s top speed depends on the model, the controller setting, the rider’s weight and the terrain. Published figures differ between models, so check the manufacturer’s numbers for the exact bike.","intro":["How fast a Sur-Ron goes depends on the model, the riding mode, the gearing, the rider’s weight and the surface. The number in a specification is a best-case figure, and real-world speed and range are usually lower.","This guide explains what actually decides speed and range so you can read a specification sensibly. These bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"What decides top speed","ul":["The motor’s power and the controller’s limits.","The riding mode selected on the bike.","Gearing: the front and rear sprocket sizes change how the power is used.","Rider weight, terrain and wind.","Tyres and tyre pressure."]},{"h":"Why range is never one number","p":["Battery capacity sets the energy you start with, but how you ride sets how quickly it is used. Fast riding, steep hills, soft sand and heavy riders all shorten range. Riding gently in a low power mode stretches it.","A useful habit is to plan rides around half of the maker’s quoted range until you learn how your bike behaves."]},{"h":"Reading a specification","p":["Look at the battery voltage and capacity, the motor’s continuous and peak power, the weight and the gearing. Compare the same measures across models rather than relying on top speed alone. If a number is missing or unclear, ask the seller or the manufacturer before you order."]},{"h":"Gearing and sprocket changes","p":["Changing the front or rear sprocket changes how the motor’s power is used. A larger rear sprocket (or smaller front) gives stronger acceleration and a lower top speed, and the reverse gives a higher top speed with less acceleration. Match the change to how you ride, and keep the chain aligned and in good condition: see the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Riding modes and power delivery","p":["Most electric dirt bikes offer riding modes that limit power. Use a lower mode while you learn the bike and on tight terrain, and a higher mode only when you are confident and in a suitable open area. Modes also stretch the range."]},{"h":"Speed and the rules","p":["Speed on private property is your responsibility, and these bikes are not road legal in Australia. See the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Wear full protective gear every time you ride."]},{"h":"Planning a ride around real range","p":["Range depends on how you ride more than on the figure in the brochure. Gentle trail riding in a low mode can stretch a battery, while repeated hard acceleration, hill climbs and sand drain it quickly. A useful approach is to ride your first few sessions with the battery display visible, note how much charge a typical ride uses, then plan future rides with a margin.","If you regularly run out of charge before you want to stop, a second battery or a larger pack is more useful than chasing a higher top speed."]},{"h":"Keeping the bike safe at higher speeds","ul":["Check brake pads and rotor condition before every ride.","Keep tyres at a pressure suited to the surface.","Check the chain, sprockets and axle bolts regularly.","Wear full protective gear every time, including body armour.","Ride within your ability and in areas where you can stop safely."]},{"h":"Common myths about electric dirt bike speed","ul":["“Top speed is what matters.” For most trail riding, torque delivery and control matter more.","“More power always means more fun.” More power needs more skill, better brakes and better gear.","“The advertised range is what you will get.” Real range depends heavily on terrain and rider.","“All modes are the same.” Modes can change power, throttle response and sometimes speed limits."]},{"h":"Choosing settings for different rides","p":["A low mode suits learning, tight trails and long rides. A medium mode suits general trail riding. A high mode suits open tracks and experienced riders. Switch modes only when the bike is stopped or you are in a safe, open area, and learn how the bike behaves in each mode."]},{"h":"Questions to ask about any figure you read online","ul":["Which model year and version is it about?","Was it measured on a flat road or on a trail?","What was the rider’s weight and the mode used?","Was the speed measured by GPS, or read from the display?","Who measured it, and could someone else repeat it?"]},{"h":"Going faster is a safety decision","p":["Higher speed means longer stopping distances and more severe crashes. Upgrading power without upgrading brakes and protective gear is a poor trade. See our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/).","Compare the full range in the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) and browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How fast does a Sur-Ron go?","a":"Top speed varies by model, controller mode, rider weight and surface, so there is no single figure. Use the manufacturer’s published speed for your exact model as the reference, and remember the bikes are for off-road and private property use only."},{"q":"What affects a Sur-Ron’s range?","a":"Range changes with battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, temperature and how hard the throttle is used. Riding in a lower power mode and on flatter ground stretches range. Carry a spare battery if you plan a long day. See the sur-ron range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where can I see current Sur-Ron models and prices?","a":"The Sur-Ron brand page lists every model currently available with Australian-dollar prices, and each product page links to the relevant parts. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want help choosing between models. Open the sur-ron category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a faster bike always better?","a":"Not always. A very fast bike is harder to control for newer riders and uses battery quickly. Choose a model and power mode that matches your skill, your terrain and the protective gear you own. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the sur-ron range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Sur-Ron bikes and spares?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Sur-Ron bikes and spares in the sur-ron category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"how fast do a surron go","supporting":["how fast does a surron go","surron ultra bee range","how fast do a surron go guide","how fast do a surron go explained","how fast do a surron go for beginners"]},"words":686},"talaria-electric-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"Talaria makes electric off-road bikes including the Sting, Komodo and XXX. This guide compares the models listed at Torque E-Bikes by rider type and price.","intro":["Talaria builds a range of electric off-road bikes, and the model names (Sting, XXX, Komodo and others) cover frames with different power levels and rider sizes. Choosing between them comes down to how you ride and how experienced you are.","This guide explains the families, what to compare and how Talaria bikes sit alongside Sur-Ron. Bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"The Talaria families","ul":["**Sting:** performance off-road models in several versions.","**XXX:** a full-size model with its own range, plus the smaller XXX Mini.","**Komodo:** another model line in the range.","**Junior models:** smaller bikes for younger riders."],"p":["Names and versions change by model year. See the current Talaria range on our [Talaria brand page](/brands/talaria/)."]},{"h":"What to compare","ul":["Motor power and the riding modes available.","Battery voltage and capacity.","Weight, seat height and suspension.","Rider height and experience.","Parts and upgrade support."]},{"h":"Talaria or Sur-Ron?","p":["They are different brands with different models, so compare like for like: motor, battery, weight and price for a similar rider and use. Our [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/) covers the other side of that comparison."]},{"h":"Choosing by rider and terrain","p":["A lighter Talaria suits smaller riders and tighter trails, while a larger model suits taller riders and harder terrain. Check seat height and rider size, and think about the surface you ride most. Ask the team if you want help comparing two models."]},{"h":"Parts, upgrades and servicing","p":["Before you buy any electric bike, check that spare parts such as batteries, chargers and brake pads can be bought in Australia. For Talaria bikes, match each part to the exact model and year, and have drivetrain, brakes and tyres checked regularly. See our [chain care](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/) and [brakes](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) guides."]},{"h":"How to compare two Talaria models fairly","p":["Put the two models side by side and compare the same measures: continuous and peak motor power, battery voltage and capacity, weight, seat height, suspension travel, brakes and what is included in the box. Then compare the price in Australian dollars, including freight and any extras you need such as a charger or protective gear.","Do not compare a model from one year with another from a different year without checking what changed. Ask the seller to confirm the exact model year and specification before you order."]},{"h":"Servicing and parts","p":["Keep a small kit of wear items: brake pads, a chain and sprockets, tyres and spare bolts. Check them regularly and replace them before they fail. Match every part to the exact model and year, and keep a record of what you have fitted so ordering later is easier. See our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Riding, charging and battery care for a Talaria","p":["Electric dirt bikes put heavy demands on batteries, so look after yours. Use the supplied charger, let the battery cool after a hard ride before charging, and avoid leaving it fully charged for long periods. Keep the contacts clean, and store the battery somewhere cool and dry. If the bike will sit for several weeks, leave the battery partly charged and check it from time to time.","Learn how the bike behaves in each riding mode, and use the lowest mode when you are on unfamiliar ground. Replace worn tyres, brake pads and chains before they fail."]},{"h":"Gear, rules and where to ride","p":["Wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour, and ride only on private property or approved off-road areas. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Which Talaria is best for a beginner?** A lighter model in a low mode is usually easier to learn on.","**Can I use a Talaria on the road?** No. They are for off-road and private property use only.","**Where can I get parts?** Check the parts available locally before buying, and match each part to the model and year.","**How should I care for the battery?** Follow the manual, charge in a ventilated place and store it partly charged."]},{"h":"Looking for a model that is not listed?","p":["Some Talaria models people search for, such as the X3, MX3 and MX4, are not currently listed at Torque E-Bikes. Our [Talaria X3, MX3 and MX4 guide](/blog/talaria-x3-mx3-and-mx4/) explains how they fit in the range, and you can message the team to ask what can be sourced.","For everything else, browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Talaria Sting and Komodo?","a":"The Sting is Talaria’s performance-oriented off-road line, while the Komodo is a different model with its own frame and specification. Compare the manufacturer’s published motor, battery and weight figures for the model year you are considering. See the talaria range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are Talaria bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"No. Talaria electric dirt bikes are off-road machines and are not road legal or registrable in Australia. Ride them only on private property or in approved off-road areas, wearing full protective gear. Browse the talaria category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which Talaria models does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The Talaria brand page shows the current models, including Sting and Komodo variants and the XXX. Prices are in Australian dollars and shipping is arranged Australia-wide before payment. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a Talaria better than a Sur-Ron?","a":"They are different brands with different models, so compare like for like: motor, battery, weight, suspension and price. Both are off-road bikes. Read the model pages side by side and decide by the terrain and riding style you have in mind."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list Talaria bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists Talaria bikes in the talaria category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order. Open the talaria category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"talaria triple x","supporting":["talaria xx","talaria triple x guide","how to choose talaria triple x","talaria triple x explained","talaria triple x for beginners"]},"words":668},"electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide":{"hook":"An electric mountain bike is a mountain bike with a pedal-assist motor and battery. Hardtails suit smoother trails and budgets, while full-suspension e-MTBs suit rough terrain.","intro":["An electric mountain bike (e-MTB) is a mountain bike with a pedal-assist motor and battery. The motor adds power as you pedal, which makes climbs easier and lets you ride further on trails.","This guide covers the choices that matter most: hardtail or full suspension, motor and battery, brakes and frame size."],"sections":[{"h":"Hardtail or full suspension","p":["A hardtail has suspension only at the front. It is simpler, lighter and cheaper to maintain, and suits smoother trails and tighter budgets. A full-suspension bike has a rear shock as well, giving more grip and comfort on rough ground at a higher price and with more maintenance."]},{"h":"Motor and battery","p":["Mid-drive motors sit at the cranks and use the bike’s gears, and they usually feel natural on climbs. Hub motors sit in a wheel and are common on lower-priced bikes. Battery capacity decides range: larger batteries add range and weight. Choose a capacity that covers your longest regular ride."]},{"h":"Brakes, wheels and frame size","ul":["Hydraulic disc brakes give strong, controllable stopping on descents.","Wheel size (27.5 or 29 inch) affects handling and the bike’s fit for your height.","Frame size should match your height and reach, so check the maker’s size chart."]},{"h":"Where you can ride","p":["Trail access rules vary by land manager and state, and some trails restrict e-bikes. Check with the land manager before you ride. For road and path rules, see our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Climbing, descending and the right assist level","p":["Most e-MTBs offer several assist levels. Use a lower level to save battery on flatter sections and a higher one on climbs. On descents the motor does nothing, so the bike’s weight and brakes matter more than on a normal bike. Practise braking early and smoothly, and keep your weight back on steep sections."]},{"h":"Maintenance for trail bikes","ul":["Clean the drivetrain after muddy or dusty rides.","Check brake pads and rotors often, because e-bikes wear them faster.","Keep tyre pressure suited to the trail and your weight.","Store and charge the battery as the maker recommends."]},{"h":"Transporting an e-MTB","p":["E-MTBs are heavy, so check the weight rating of any car rack before you buy, and remove the battery before lifting if you can. Secure the bike properly and never leave a lithium battery in a hot car."]},{"h":"Motors, sensors and battery integration","p":["E-MTB motors are usually mid-drive units that sit at the cranks. Torque sensors respond to how hard you pedal, which makes power delivery smooth and proportional on technical climbs, while cadence sensors respond to pedalling speed and can feel less natural. Batteries are either integrated into the frame, which looks clean and lowers weight, or mounted externally, which is often easier to swap.","Check how easy it is to remove the battery for charging, and whether the maker sells spare batteries and chargers. Look at the display too: you want to see assist level, speed and battery charge clearly in sunlight."]},{"h":"Sizing, geometry and suspension setup","p":["Pick the frame size that matches your height and reach, then set the suspension sag using the maker’s guide. Too little sag makes the bike harsh and too much makes it wallow. Adjust tyre pressure to suit your weight and the trail, and take time to learn how the bike handles with the extra weight of the motor and battery."]},{"h":"Budget, value and what to prioritise","p":["Spend first on the frame, brakes and motor, because those are hard to change later. Tyres, grips, saddles and pedals can be upgraded cheaply. A hardtail with good brakes and a reliable motor often beats a full-suspension bike with poor components at the same price. Check the warranty on the motor and battery, because these are the most expensive parts.","Look at weight too. E-MTBs are heavy, and a few kilograms can change how easy the bike is to handle and lift. If you will carry the bike up steps or onto a car, check the weight and the battery’s removability."]},{"h":"Trail etiquette and safety on an e-MTB","ul":["Give way to walkers and slower riders, and slow down on shared trails.","Do not use high assist levels on narrow, crowded trails.","Follow local trail signage and e-bike access rules.","Wear a full-face or trail helmet with gloves and knee protection.","Carry a repair kit, water and a phone."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need a special helmet?** A trail or enduro helmet gives more coverage than a road helmet, and many riders choose a full-face helmet for steep descents.","**Will an e-MTB ruin my fitness?** Many riders find they ride more often and for longer, and still get a workout.","**How long do batteries last?** They lose capacity gradually over years, depending on use and care.","**Can I ride an e-MTB on any trail?** No. Check local access rules, because some trails restrict e-bikes."]},{"h":"Compare models","p":["Browse [electric mountain bikes](/shop/electric-mountain-bikes/) and compare two or three in the same price band on motor, battery, suspension and brakes. For all types in one place, see the [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between an e-MTB and a normal mountain bike?","a":"An e-MTB adds a pedal-assist motor and battery to a mountain bike frame, so power is added as you pedal. It weighs more but makes climbs and long rides easier. Brakes and suspension are usually stronger to handle the extra weight and speed."},{"q":"Can I ride an e-MTB on any trail?","a":"Not always. Some trails restrict or ban e-bikes, and rules vary by land manager and state. Check the trail owner’s rules before you ride, and keep to the speed and etiquette rules posted for shared tracks. See the electric mountain bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where can I see e-mountain bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The electric mountain bikes category lists every e-MTB currently available with Australian-dollar prices. Filter by brand to compare models, and message the team on WhatsApp if you want help with frame sizing. Open the electric mountain bikes category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Is a hardtail e-MTB good enough for most riders?","a":"A hardtail suits smoother trails and a tighter budget, and it is simpler to maintain. A full-suspension bike is more comfortable on rough, technical terrain. Choose by the trails you ride most often. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the electric mountain bikes range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric mountain bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric mountain bikes in the electric mountain bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"mountain e cycle","supporting":["electric mountain","mountain e cycle guide","how to choose mountain e cycle","mountain e cycle explained","mountain e cycle for beginners"]},"words":778},"folding-electric-bikes":{"hook":"A folding e-bike is an electric bike with a hinged frame that folds for storage and transport. It suits small homes, car boots and mixed-mode commuting.","intro":["A folding electric bike has a hinged frame that folds down for storage and transport. It suits small flats, car boots and mixed commutes that include trains or buses.","The trade-off is that folding bikes usually have smaller wheels, so they can feel less stable and less comfortable than full-size bikes on rough surfaces."],"sections":[{"h":"Who folding e-bikes suit","ul":["People with limited storage at home or work.","Commuters who combine riding with trains, buses or a car.","Riders who want a bike they can carry up stairs, within the weight they can lift."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Folded size: does it fit your boot, cupboard or desk space?","Weight: e-bikes are heavy, so check you can lift it when folded.","Fold mechanism: look for a secure latch and a quick, simple fold.","Battery: removable batteries are easier to charge indoors.","Brakes and tyres: small wheels need good brakes and decent tyres."]},{"h":"Folding or full size?","p":["A folding e-bike trades some ride comfort and range for portability. If you ride mostly on flat paths and need to store or carry the bike, a folder works well. If you ride long distances or on rough roads, a full-size commuter may be better: see our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Rules and safety","p":["Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W standard can use roads and paths, subject to state rules. Wear an approved helmet, and use lights at night: see our [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/)."]},{"h":"Riding comfort and handling","p":["Smaller wheels make a folding bike nimble but twitchier, and they feel bumps more. Check the tyre width, the frame stiffness and the handlebar height, and set the saddle and stem so your position is comfortable. A suspension seat post can add comfort on rough roads."]},{"h":"Taking a folding e-bike on public transport","p":["Rules for taking bikes on trains and buses differ between operators, and some restrict e-bikes or limit battery size. Check the operator’s rules before you travel, and remove the battery if you are allowed to carry it separately."]},{"h":"Care and storage","p":["Keep the hinge and latch clean and lightly lubricated, and check they are fully locked before every ride. Store the battery indoors at a moderate temperature, and fold the bike carefully so cables are not pinched."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before buying a folding e-bike","ul":["How big is it when folded, and will it fit my storage space?","How much does it weigh with the battery, and can I lift it?","Is the battery removable and can it be charged indoors?","What wheel size and tyre width does it use?","How long does the fold take, and does the latch feel secure?","Are replacement parts such as tyres and brake pads easy to get?"]},{"h":"Who should look at a full-size bike instead","p":["If your ride is longer than about 10 to 15 km each way, rough in places, or you carry loads often, a full-size commuter or cargo bike will usually be more comfortable and stable. Folding bikes shine when storage or transport is the main problem, not when you want a longer-range tourer. See the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/) for the alternative."]},{"h":"Using a folding e-bike in daily life","p":["A folding e-bike can live under a desk, in a cupboard or in a car boot, and can ride with you on a train. To make it practical, practise folding and unfolding it until it takes a minute or less. Learn where to lift it and how to carry it, remove the battery for charging indoors, and keep a bike bag or cover for dirty wheels."]},{"h":"Servicing small wheels and hinges","ul":["Check the hinge and latch for play before every ride.","Check tyre pressure often, because small tyres lose pressure quickly.","Lubricate the hinge sparingly.","Watch for wear on the chain and cassette, because small wheels spin faster."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Are folding e-bikes slower?** Not necessarily, but small wheels feel less stable at speed.","**Can I take one on a train?** Rules differ by operator. Check the operator’s rules for e-bikes and batteries.","**How heavy are they?** Often heavier than a folding bike without a motor, so check the weight before buying.","**Can I use one for long rides?** You can, but a larger bike is usually more comfortable on long trips."]},{"h":"Browse folding e-bikes","p":["See current models in the [folding e-bikes](/shop/folding-e-bikes/) category, and compare prices on the [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are folding e-bikes good for commuting?","a":"Yes for many riders. They fold small enough for trains, lifts and cupboards, and the motor helps with hills. They weigh more than a normal folding bike, so check the weight if you carry it up stairs. See the folding e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How small does a folding e-bike fold?","a":"Folded size varies by model, so use the dimensions on each product page. Smaller wheels fold more compactly but ride differently from full-size wheels. Measure your storage space and your car boot before ordering. Browse the folding e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which folding e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The folding e-bikes category lists models including fat-tyre and step-through folders. Compare prices and weights on each page, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you need help choosing. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a folding e-bike as good as a full-size one?","a":"It trades some ride comfort and range for portability. If you ride mostly on flat paths and need to store or carry the bike, a folder works well. For long rides or rough surfaces, a full-size e-bike is usually better."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list folding e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists folding e-bikes in the folding e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric folding cycle","supporting":["bag for foldable bike","electric folding cycle guide","how to choose electric folding cycle","electric folding cycle explained","electric folding cycle for beginners"]},"words":662},"electric-trikes-for-adults":{"hook":"An electric trike is a three-wheel e-bike that stays upright without balancing. Adult e-trikes suit riders who want stability, easier starts or load-carrying.","intro":["An electric trike has three wheels, so it stays upright when you stop and is easy to start from a standstill. That stability suits riders with balance concerns, people carrying loads and anyone who wants a relaxed ride.","This guide covers the main types, what to check and when a two-wheel cargo bike might suit better."],"sections":[{"h":"Who electric trikes suit","ul":["Riders who find balance difficult or prefer not to put a foot down.","People carrying shopping, tools or equipment.","Riders who want a stable bike at low speed."]},{"h":"Types of trike","p":["Most adult e-trikes have either two wheels at the back (a delta layout), which is common for cargo and shopping, or two at the front (a tadpole layout), which is more stable when cornering. Each has a different feel, so test a model if you can."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Load rating: check the maximum load for the rack, basket and the whole bike.","Motor and battery: choose a battery that covers your usual trips.","Turning circle and width: will it fit through your gates and doors?","Brakes: a heavier bike needs strong brakes.","Storage and charging: where will it live and charge?"]},{"h":"Trike or cargo bike?","p":["Choose a trike if stability and easy starts matter most. Choose a two-wheel cargo bike if you want a narrower bike that handles more like a normal bicycle. Our [cargo e-bike guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) covers that side of the choice."]},{"h":"Riding a trike safely","p":["A trike does not lean into corners like a bike, so slow down before turns and avoid sharp turns at speed, especially when loaded or on a camber. Learn the bike’s width so you can judge gaps, and brake smoothly. Practise in an empty car park before you ride in traffic."]},{"h":"Storage and charging","p":["A trike is wider and longer than a bike, so check that it fits your storage space and can be wheeled through doors and gates. Charge the battery in a ventilated area, and take a removable battery inside if the bike lives outdoors."]},{"h":"Who should test first","p":["If you have balance or mobility concerns, try a trike before you buy where you can. Check how easy it is to get on and off, how the brakes feel and whether you can reach the controls comfortably."]},{"h":"Features worth paying for on a trike","ul":["A low step-through for easy mounting and dismounting.","A parking brake so the trike cannot roll away.","A reverse gear or walk-assist on heavier models.","A rear basket or cargo box with a clear load rating.","Good lighting and reflectors, especially if you ride at dusk."]},{"h":"What to check on arrival","p":["Check that all bolts are tight, the brakes work evenly on all wheels, the tyres are inflated and the battery charges properly. Take a short test ride in a quiet area to learn the turning circle and how the steering feels. Keep the manual and receipt for warranty claims."]},{"h":"Choosing the right motor and battery for a trike","p":["Trikes are heavier than bikes and often carry loads, so look for a motor with good low-speed torque and a battery large enough for your usual trips. If your route is hilly or you carry heavy loads, choose a larger battery and check how the motor handles starting on a slope. Check the maker’s range figure and then reduce it for hills and loads."]},{"h":"Where electric trikes are used","ul":["Neighbourhood shopping trips.","Recreational rides on bike paths.","Delivering small loads.","Mobility for riders who prefer stability."]},{"h":"Browse the range","p":["See current models in the [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/) category, and check [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) for where you can ride."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric trikes easy to ride?","a":"Many riders find e-trikes easier than two-wheelers because they stay upright at a standstill and need no balance. They corner differently, so take wide turns and slow down on slopes until you are used to the handling. See the cargo bikes & e-trikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can an electric trike carry a passenger or shopping?","a":"Many e-trikes have a rear basket or cargo bed, but load limits vary. Check the stated maximum load on the product page, and keep weight low and evenly spread for stable handling. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which electric trikes does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The cargo bikes and e-trikes category lists folding, step-through and cargo trikes from several brands. Prices and features are on each product page, and the team can help match a trike to your needs. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Should I choose an e-trike or a cargo bike?","a":"Choose a trike if stability and easy starts matter most. Choose a two-wheel cargo bike if you want a more bicycle-like ride and can balance a loaded bike. Think about where you will park it and how narrow your paths are."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric trikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric trikes in the cargo bikes & e-trikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"3 wheel electric bike","supporting":["3 wheel cycle","adult trike","adult 3 wheel bike","kids 3 wheel bike","mobility trike"]},"words":550},"electric-cargo-bikes":{"hook":"An electric cargo bike is an e-bike built to carry loads, children or shopping. Longtail and box designs trade length and handling for capacity.","intro":["An electric cargo bike carries shopping, tools, equipment or children with the help of a motor. For short local trips it can replace many car journeys, but it needs more space and a stronger frame than a normal bike.","This guide covers the main types and what to check before you choose."],"sections":[{"h":"Longtail and front-loader types","p":["A longtail has an extended rear rack and handles much like a normal bike, so it is a good first cargo bike. A front-loader carries a box or tray ahead of the rider and suits bulky loads, but it is longer and takes more learning."]},{"h":"Load ratings and safety","ul":["Check the maximum load for the whole bike and for the rack or box.","Fit appropriate child seats and restraints if you carry children.","Check the brakes: loaded bikes need strong, reliable braking.","Check that the stand holds the loaded bike upright."]},{"h":"Motor and battery","p":["Loads and hills use more battery, so choose a larger battery than you would for an unloaded bike. A motor that gives good low-speed torque helps with starting and climbing. Real range is lower with a heavy load."]},{"h":"Is a cargo e-bike a car replacement?","p":["For short local trips with shopping or children, it can be. It will not replace a car for long distances or large loads. Think about your typical week before deciding, and consider a [trike](/blog/electric-trikes-for-adults/) if stability matters more than a narrow bike."]},{"h":"Carrying children safely","p":["Only carry children in seats or boxes designed for the purpose, and use the restraints and helmets the maker specifies. Check the minimum age and weight for any child seat, and never exceed the bike’s load rating. Practise riding the loaded bike before you ride in traffic."]},{"h":"Parking, locking and storage","p":["Cargo bikes are long and heavy, so check where you will park and store the bike, and use a strong lock: see the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/). Many riders fit a robust stand so the bike stays upright while loading."]},{"h":"Costs to plan for","p":["Allow for a good lock, child seats or a box, panniers, lights and servicing. Brake pads and tyres wear faster on a heavy bike, so budget for replacements."]},{"h":"Choosing a longtail or a front-loader","p":["A longtail keeps the load behind the rider and handles much like a normal bike, which makes it a good first cargo bike. A front-loader puts the load ahead of the rider so you can see passengers and cargo, but it is longer and has a steering linkage that takes a little getting used to. Think about where you will park and ride: narrow paths and tight garages suit a longtail better."]},{"h":"Brakes, tyres and maintenance on a heavy bike","ul":["Choose hydraulic disc brakes with rotors large enough for the load.","Check brake pads often, because they wear faster under load.","Use tyres with a good load rating and keep pressure correct.","Check spokes and wheel true regularly.","Keep the chain clean and lubricated."]},{"h":"Planning a week with a cargo e-bike","p":["Think about the trips you make each week: school runs, shopping, errands and visits. Add up the distances and the loads, and check that a cargo bike can handle them. Think about parking and security at each destination, and how you will charge the battery. If most trips are short and local, a cargo bike can replace many car journeys."]},{"h":"Accessories that make a cargo bike more useful","ul":["Rain covers for passengers and loads.","Child seats and harnesses made for the bike.","Strong stands and locks.","Lights and reflectors for visibility.","Panniers and boxes suited to the frame."]},{"h":"Browse and compare","p":["See current models in the [cargo bikes and e-trikes](/shop/cargo-bikes-and-e-trikes/) category and read our [bike racks and trailers guide](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) for add-ons."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How much can an electric cargo bike carry?","a":"Capacity depends on the model, so use the maximum load stated on each product page. Distribute weight low and evenly, and check that brakes and tyres suit the load you plan to carry. See the cargo bikes & e-trikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I carry children on an electric cargo bike?","a":"Many cargo bikes support child seats or benches, but seat type and age limits vary by model and state. Use seats designed for your bike, fit helmets and follow the manufacturer’s limits. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where do I find cargo e-bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"Open the cargo bikes and e-trikes category to see every cargo model with Australian-dollar prices. Brand pages list each maker’s range, and the team can help compare models. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a cargo e-bike a good car replacement?","a":"For short local trips with shopping or children, it can be. It will not replace a car for long distances or heavy goods. Count the trips you make each week and the distances before deciding. Browse the cargo bikes & e-trikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list cargo e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists cargo e-bikes in the cargo bikes & e-trikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e bike with cargo trailer","supporting":["e bike with cargo trailer guide","how to choose e bike with cargo trailer","e bike with cargo trailer explained","e bike with cargo trailer for beginners","e bike with cargo trailer tips"]},"words":558},"what-is-an-e-moto":{"hook":"An e-moto is an electric motorbike-style machine with a throttle and no pedal-assist limit. In Australia, e-motos are for off-road and private property use only.","intro":["An e-moto is an electric motorbike: a battery-powered two-wheeler that looks and rides more like a motorbike than a bicycle. Many have pedals removed or none at all, and they use a throttle rather than pedal assist.","The term covers everything from small kids’ bikes to powerful off-road machines. Understanding how an e-moto differs from an e-bike matters because the rules are different."],"sections":[{"h":"E-moto, e-bike and electric dirt bike","ul":["**E-bike:** a bicycle with a pedal-assist motor. A 250W pedal-assist e-bike can use public roads, subject to state rules.","**E-moto:** an electric motorbike with a throttle and a more powerful motor.","**Electric dirt bike:** an e-moto built for off-road riding."]},{"h":"What the rules say","p":["In Australia the e-motos sold for off-road riding are for off-road and private property use only. They are not road legal and cannot simply be registered for the road. Read our guide to [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you buy."]},{"h":"Why riders choose e-motos","ul":["Quiet, with no fuel, clutch or gear changes.","Less routine maintenance than a petrol engine.","Instant power delivery, which needs respect."]},{"h":"E-moto or e-bike?","p":["Choose an e-bike if you want to ride on roads and paths with pedal assist. Choose an e-moto if you want to ride off-road on private land. Our [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/) and [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) cover each side."]},{"h":"How an e-moto is built","p":["An e-moto uses a larger battery and a more powerful motor than an e-bike, often with a chain or belt drive to the rear wheel, motorbike-style suspension and a controller that offers several riding modes. Many have hydraulic disc brakes and wide, knobbly tyres for off-road riding."]},{"h":"Choosing your first e-moto","ul":["Pick a lighter bike with a low-power mode.","Check the seat height and rider weight limit.","Buy full protective gear with the bike.","Plan where you will ride before you buy."]},{"h":"Charging and care","p":["Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and do not leave it charging unattended. Look after the chain, brakes and tyres as you would on a petrol bike. Read the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"What an e-moto is and is not","p":["An e-moto is not a bicycle with a bigger motor. It is a different class of vehicle, and the way it is built, sold and regulated reflects that. Most are sold for off-road and private-property use. Even if a seller describes a model as “street legal” elsewhere in the world, it does not mean it meets Australian rules, so check before you assume."]},{"h":"Learning to ride an e-moto","ul":["Start in a low-power mode on flat, open ground.","Practise smooth throttle and brake control before riding hills.","Learn how regenerative braking, if fitted, feels.","Always wear a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and armour.","Consider a rider training course at a licensed venue."]},{"h":"Choosing between e-moto brands and sizes","p":["E-motos range from kids’ models to powerful adult bikes. Compare weight, seat height, motor power and battery capacity, and choose a bike that suits the rider’s size and experience. A lighter bike is easier to handle and pick up, and a heavier one can feel more stable at speed. Think about where you will ride and how you will transport the bike."]},{"h":"Costs and ownership","ul":["Purchase price and freight for a bike with a lithium battery.","Protective gear for each rider.","Replacement chains, sprockets, tyres and brake pads.","A safe place to charge and store the bike.","Insurance, if your policy allows it."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Is an e-moto the same as an electric scooter?** No. A scooter is a different, usually smaller vehicle, and e-motos here mean motorbike-style off-road bikes.","**Can I register an e-moto?** Off-road e-motos sold here are for private property use only and are not built to be registered for the road.","**Is an e-moto good for kids?** Kids’ models exist, with power limits, but supervision and protective gear are essential.","**How long does the battery last?** It depends on battery size, mode and terrain."]},{"h":"Safety first","p":["Wear a certified helmet and protective gear, learn on a lower-powered bike and ride only where it is permitted. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What does e-moto mean?","a":"E-moto is short for electric motorbike. It describes a motorbike-style machine powered by a battery and electric motor, usually without pedals. In Australia, high-powered e-motos are for off-road and private property use only. See the electric dirt bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Is an e-moto the same as an electric dirt bike?","a":"Often, yes. Electric dirt bikes are a type of e-moto designed for off-road riding. Some e-motos are built for tracks, trails or kids, so compare the model and intended use before buying. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes sell e-motos?","a":"Yes. The electric dirt bikes and kids electric dirt bikes categories list e-motos from several brands. Each page states whether the bike is for off-road and private property use only. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I buy an e-moto or an e-bike?","a":"Choose an e-bike if you want to ride on roads and paths with pedal assist. Choose an e-moto if you want motorbike-style riding on private land or off-road tracks. Consider where you can legally ride before you decide. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-motos and electric motorbikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-motos and electric motorbikes in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"emoto","supporting":["emoto guide","how to choose emoto","emoto explained","emoto for beginners","emoto tips"]},"words":630},"helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes":{"hook":"The right helmet depends on how you ride. E-bike commuters need a certified bike helmet, while electric dirt bike riders need a full-face motocross helmet plus goggles, gloves and body armour.","intro":["A certified helmet is the single most important piece of riding gear, and gloves, goggles and armour add protection that matters even more on an electric dirt bike. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so crashes can be more serious.","This guide covers what to wear for each type of riding and how to check that gear fits."],"sections":[{"h":"Helmets: look for a standard and a good fit","p":["Choose a helmet that meets the standard for the type of riding. In Australia, bicycle helmets are expected to meet AS/NZS 2063, and motocross-style helmets for off-road bikes meet their own standards. Check the label, and check that it is the right type for your riding.","A helmet should sit level, snug but not painful, with the straps adjusted so it does not move when you shake your head. Replace a helmet after a significant impact."]},{"h":"What to wear for each type of riding","ul":["**City and commuting:** an approved bicycle helmet, gloves, closed shoes and lights or reflective items.","**Mountain biking:** a helmet with good coverage, gloves, eye protection and knee or elbow pads if the trail is rough.","**Electric dirt bikes:** a motocross helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour or a chest protector."]},{"h":"Gloves, goggles and boots","p":["Gloves protect your hands in a fall and reduce vibration. Goggles keep dust and debris out of your eyes. Boots protect your ankles and feet on an off-road bike. Choose gear that fits well so it does not slip or restrict movement."]},{"h":"Is a smart helmet worth it?","p":["A smart helmet adds features such as lights or audio, which can help visibility and navigation. It still has to meet the same safety standard and fit properly, so choose on safety first and features second."]},{"h":"Fit and care","ul":["Try helmets on and walk around the shop or at home before you commit.","Adjust the retention system so the helmet does not rock.","Keep the helmet away from heat and chemicals.","Replace it after a significant impact, even if it looks fine."]},{"h":"Gear for kids","p":["Children need gear that fits them now, not gear to grow into. A loose helmet or oversized armour can do more harm than good. See the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for the gear to buy with a first bike."]},{"h":"Visibility on the road","p":["If you ride a road-legal e-bike at dusk or night, add lights and reflective gear so other road users can see you. See the [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/)."]},{"h":"How to choose between helmet types","p":["A bicycle helmet is light and ventilated, and it is the right choice for road-legal e-bikes and for commuting. A mountain bike helmet adds coverage at the back and sides. A motocross-style helmet with a peak and chin bar offers more protection for off-road riding and is the usual choice on electric dirt bikes. Pick the helmet designed for your type of riding, and do not use a helmet made for a different sport."]},{"h":"Body armour, boots and gloves","ul":["Chest and back protectors reduce injury from falls and flying debris.","Knee and elbow guards help on rough ground.","Boots with ankle support protect your feet and shins.","Gloves with reinforced palms protect your hands and improve grip."]},{"h":"Helmet fit in detail","p":["Measure your head about a finger-width above the eyebrows, and use the maker’s size chart. The helmet should sit level on your head with the front edge about two fingers above your eyebrows. The straps should form a “V” under each ear, and the buckle should sit under the chin with room for one finger between the strap and your chin. When you shake your head, the helmet should move with it rather than shifting.","If a helmet pinches, gives pressure points or moves, try another size or shape. Different brands fit differently, so do not assume a size from one brand will match another."]},{"h":"When to replace gear","ul":["Replace a helmet after a significant impact, even if there is no visible damage.","Replace helmets that are cracked, deformed or very old.","Replace gloves with worn palms or loose stitching.","Replace goggles with scratched lenses.","Replace armour that is cracked or no longer fits."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need a different helmet for e-bikes?** A standard bicycle helmet is the legal minimum for road-legal e-bikes, but faster or off-road riding may call for more coverage.","**How often should I replace a helmet?** After an impact, and when it is old or damaged. Check the maker’s guidance.","**Is armour worth it?** For off-road riding, yes. It reduces injury in falls.","**Can I use a motorcycle helmet on a bike?** Check the standard and the rules for where you ride."]},{"h":"Browse gear","p":["See the [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/) category, and read the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) and [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) for riders buying a first bike."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What helmet should I wear on an electric dirt bike?","a":"Wear a full-face motocross helmet that fits snugly, plus goggles, gloves, boots and body armour. Check the helmet’s certification marking and replace it after any significant impact. Ride only on private property or approved off-road areas. See the helmets, gloves & protection range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How do I know my helmet fits?","a":"It should sit level, snug but not painful, with no movement when you shake your head. The chin strap should hold it firmly in place. If it moves or leaves pressure points, try another size or shape. Browse the helmets, gloves & protection category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What helmets does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The helmets, gloves and protection category lists bike helmets, kids’ helmets, motocross helmets, smart helmets, goggles and protective armour. Browse by type and check each page for sizing. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a smart helmet worth it?","a":"A smart helmet adds features such as lights or audio, which can help visibility and navigation. Fit, certification and comfort still come first. Compare the features you will use against the extra cost. Browse the helmets, gloves & protection category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list helmets and riding gear?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists helmets and riding gear in the helmets, gloves & protection category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"ebike helmet guide","supporting":["how to choose a bike helmet","bike helmet sizing","what to wear when riding an ebike","dirt bike gear for beginners","ebike protective gear guide"]},"words":745},"e-bike-lights":{"hook":"E-bike lights make you visible and let you see the path. A front light and a rear light should both be bright enough for the conditions and mounted securely.","intro":["Good lights make you visible to other road users and let you see the path. On an e-bike you travel faster and further than on a normal bike, so lights matter more.","This guide covers brightness, mounting, power and the choice between USB and wired lights."],"sections":[{"h":"Front and rear lights you need","p":["You need a white front light and a red rear light when riding at night, and rules about reflectors and lights vary by state, so check your state’s road authority. A brighter front light helps you see, and a rear light helps others see you."]},{"h":"Brightness and beam","p":["Brightness is measured in lumens. A commuter on lit streets needs less than a rider on dark paths. A beam that spreads evenly and a mount that holds steady on bumps matter as much as raw brightness."]},{"h":"USB or wired lights","ul":["**USB lights** are portable and easy to move between bikes, but need charging.","**Wired lights** run from the bike’s battery, so there is nothing extra to charge, but they need a compatible bike and careful wiring."]},{"h":"Mounting and fitting","p":["Check that the mount suits your handlebar or fork and that the light stays aligned. Fit the front light so it lights the road rather than dazzling oncoming riders."]},{"h":"Daytime running lights and flashing modes","p":["A steady or flashing light during the day improves visibility, and many riders use a low-power daytime mode to save battery. Check your state’s rules about flashing lights, and use a steady front light at night."]},{"h":"Reflectors, bells and other fittings","p":["Reflectors and a bell are required on many bikes in Australia, and rules vary by state. Check your state road authority and add reflective accessories if your bike did not come with them."]},{"h":"Battery life and charging","p":["A USB light needs charging after longer rides, so keep a spare or a small power bank on commutes. Wired lights draw from the main battery and may shorten range slightly."]},{"h":"Choosing the right front light","p":["For lit city streets a light that makes you visible is enough. For dark paths and country roads you need a light that actually lights the way, with a beam wide enough to see the edges of the path and a mount that stays steady. Check the runtime on the mode you will use, because manufacturers often quote the lowest mode."]},{"h":"Rear lights and being seen from the side","p":["A bright red rear light, ideally with a steady and a flashing mode, makes you visible from behind. Side reflectors, reflective tape and ankle bands help you be seen from the side at junctions. Keep lights clean and charged, and carry a spare if you ride regularly at night."]},{"h":"Understanding lumens, beam shape and runtime","p":["Lumens measure how much light a lamp emits, but beam shape matters just as much. A tightly focused beam can reach far but leave dark patches at the sides, and a wide beam lights more of the path but does not reach as far. For commuting on lit streets, a wide, even beam of moderate brightness is usually enough, and for dark roads and paths you want more power and a beam with a clear cut-off so oncoming riders are not dazzled.","Runtime is quoted for the lowest mode, and the maximum mode drains the battery much faster. Choose a light whose useful modes last the length of your ride, and carry a spare or a power bank for long trips."]},{"h":"Fitting and aiming your lights","ul":["Mount the front light firmly so it does not slip on bumps.","Aim it so the brightest part of the beam lands on the path 10 to 15 metres ahead.","Mount the rear light where it is not blocked by bags or mudguards.","Check that lights are visible from the side and not hidden by luggage.","Test the lights before you set off, and clean the lens regularly."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How bright should my light be?** Brighter for dark roads, lower for lit streets.","**Do I need a rear light?** Yes, at night, and it helps in the day too.","**Can I use flashing lights?** Check your state’s rules, and use a steady front light at night.","**How do I power my lights?** USB lights need charging, and wired lights run from the bike’s battery."]},{"h":"Browse lights and horns","p":["See the [lights and horns](/shop/lights-and-horns/) category, and for the whole commuting setup read our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How bright should an e-bike front light be?","a":"Brightness depends on where you ride. Lit streets need less than dark paths or trails. Choose a light with a beam pattern that lights the ground ahead without dazzling others, and check the manufacturer’s stated output. See the lights & horns range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do I need a rear light on an e-bike?","a":"Yes. A rear light makes you visible to traffic from behind, and many states require lights when riding at night. Use a steady or flashing red light and keep spare batteries or a charged USB light. Browse the lights & horns category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What lights and horns does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The lights and horns category lists headlights, rear lights and horns from several brands, including USB-rechargeable and bike-powered options. Open a product page for fitment details. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I choose USB lights or lights wired to the bike?","a":"USB lights are portable and easy to move between bikes, but need charging. Wired lights run off the bike battery and are always on hand. Pick based on how often you ride at night. Browse the lights & horns category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike lights?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike lights in the lights & horns category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"led light on bike","supporting":["headlight of bike","dirt bike headlight","led light bike headlight","electric horn for cycle","led light on bike guide"]},"words":674},"e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes":{"hook":"E-bike brakes stop a heavier, faster bike than a normal bicycle. Hydraulic disc brakes give strong, consistent stopping, while mechanical discs are simpler to adjust.","intro":["E-bikes and electric dirt bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so their brakes work harder. Good brakes are one of the most useful safety upgrades you can make.","This guide explains the main brake types, how to choose pads and rotors and when to upgrade."],"sections":[{"h":"Hydraulic or mechanical disc brakes","p":["Mechanical disc brakes use a cable and are simple to adjust. Hydraulic disc brakes use fluid, give stronger and more controllable braking with less effort and are standard on better bikes. Hydraulic systems need correct fluid and occasional bleeding."],"ul":["Never mix brake fluids: mineral oil and DOT fluid are not interchangeable.","Have a bike mechanic bleed or repair hydraulic brakes if you are unsure."]},{"h":"Pads: organic or sintered","p":["Organic (resin) pads are quieter and bite gently. Sintered (metallic) pads last longer and resist fade on long descents or heavy bikes but can be noisier. Choose by riding style and bike weight, and always use pads that match your caliper."]},{"h":"Rotor size","p":["Larger rotors give more braking power and shed heat better. Common sizes are 160 mm, 180 mm and 203 mm. Check the maximum size your fork, frame and caliper mounts allow before you buy."]},{"h":"When to upgrade","p":["If your bike is fast or heavy, upgraded brakes can improve control and reduce fade on long descents. Signs it is time: a spongy lever, long stopping distances, squealing that does not clear, or pads worn to the backing plate."]},{"h":"Bedding in new pads and rotors","p":["New pads and rotors need bedding in to give full power. Follow the maker’s instructions: it usually involves a series of firm stops from moderate speed without locking the wheel. Avoid contaminating pads or rotors with oil or lubricant, and clean rotors with a suitable rotor cleaner."]},{"h":"Inspecting and adjusting your brakes","ul":["Check pad thickness and replace pads before they reach the backing plate.","Check rotors for warping, scoring and thickness.","Check lever feel: it should be firm, not spongy.","Check cable and hose condition."]},{"h":"Brakes on a high-power bike","p":["A bike that goes faster or weighs more needs more braking power, larger rotors and good pads. Do not upgrade the motor without checking the brakes."]},{"h":"Brake fade, heat and long descents","p":["Braking turns movement into heat, and heavy e-bikes make a lot of it. If pads and rotors overheat, braking power falls: this is brake fade. Larger rotors, sintered pads and a technique of braking in short, firm bursts instead of dragging the lever all help. If you ride long descents, check that your brakes can handle the load, and carry spare pads."]},{"h":"A basic brake service routine","ul":["Check pad thickness and replace pads when they are thin.","Check the rotor for wear, warping and contamination.","Check lever feel and travel before every ride.","Check caliper bolts and wheel axle bolts.","Have hydraulic brakes bled by a mechanic if the lever feels spongy."]},{"h":"Common brake problems and what they mean","ul":["**Squealing:** contaminated pads or rotors, or glazed pads. Clean or replace them.","**Spongy lever:** air in a hydraulic system. Have it bled.","**Weak braking:** worn pads, glazed rotors or a stretched cable.","**Rubbing:** a misaligned caliper or a bent rotor.","**Grinding:** pads worn down to the backing plate. Stop riding and replace them."]},{"h":"Choosing brakes for different kinds of e-bike","p":["A light commuter e-bike may work well with two-piston hydraulic brakes and 160 mm rotors. A heavier cargo bike or a fast e-MTB needs four-piston brakes and larger rotors. Dirt bikes use powerful calipers sized for the speed and weight. Check that your frame and fork allow the rotor size you want, and that the adaptor fits.","If you are unsure about brake upgrades, ask a bike mechanic. Brakes are safety-critical, so correct installation matters. For tyres that match, read the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How often should I replace pads?** Check them regularly and replace them when they are thin. E-bikes wear pads faster than normal bikes.","**Can I fit bigger rotors?** Only if your frame, fork and caliper mounts allow it, using the right adaptors.","**Do I need to bleed hydraulic brakes?** Occasionally. Have it done if the lever feels spongy or the brakes lose power.","**Can I mix brake parts?** Use parts made to work together, and never mix brake fluids."]},{"h":"Browse brakes","p":["See the [brakes](/shop/brakes/) category. If you are adding power with a kit, read the [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/), and for off-road bikes read the [Sur-Ron speed guide](/blog/how-fast-does-a-sur-ron-go/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How often should I replace e-bike brake pads?","a":"Replace pads when the friction material is thin, the brake squeals constantly or stopping power drops. E-bikes wear pads faster because of their weight and speed, so check them regularly and replace in pairs. See the brakes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are hydraulic brakes better than mechanical ones?","a":"Hydraulic brakes usually give stronger, more consistent stopping and lighter lever feel, which suits heavier e-bikes. Mechanical brakes are simpler to adjust and repair. Choose by how you ride and how easy servicing needs to be. Browse the brakes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What brake parts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The brakes category lists hydraulic brake sets, pads, rotors, levers, hoses and brake assemblies. Check each product page for the model it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I upgrade my brakes on a high-power bike?","a":"If your bike is fast or heavy, upgraded brakes can improve control and reduce fade on long descents. Match the upgrade to the bike’s mounting standard and rotor size, and bed the pads in before riding hard. Browse the brakes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list brake pads and rotors?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists brake pads and rotors in the brakes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"bicycle brakes","supporting":["disc brakes bike","disc brake cycle","hydraulic brakes bike","e bike brake pads","ebike brakes"]},"words":680},"bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers":{"hook":"Racks, baskets, panniers and trailers add carrying capacity to a bike, while towbar and car racks carry the whole bike by vehicle.","intro":["A rear rack, basket or trailer turns an e-bike into a practical vehicle for shopping, commuting and carrying equipment. Because e-bikes are heavy, any rack or trailer has to be strong and securely fitted.","This guide covers the main options and what to check, including a note on moving a heavy e-bike by car."],"sections":[{"h":"Racks, baskets and panniers","ul":["**Rear racks** hold panniers or a box and need mounting points on the frame.","**Front baskets** are convenient for light items but affect steering when loaded.","**Panniers** keep the bike narrow and agile."]},{"h":"Check the load limit and mounting","p":["Check the rack’s maximum load and that the bike’s frame is rated for it. Confirm that your bike has the right mounting points, and that the rack does not interfere with brakes, mudguards or lights."]},{"h":"Trailers","p":["A trailer carries more and bulkier loads than panniers but needs more space and care when turning and parking. Check the trailer’s load limit, its hitch fit and the bike’s ability to pull it safely."]},{"h":"Moving an e-bike by car","p":["Car racks need to carry the bike’s weight, and many e-bikes are heavier than a rack is rated for. Check the rack’s rated load per bike, and remove the battery before lifting where possible. A car-boot or towbar rack is a separate product category from the bike racks and bags we list."]},{"h":"Choosing panniers and bags","p":["Choose panniers that fit your rack and have a secure clip, and keep the heaviest items low and near the centre of the bike. Waterproof panniers protect the load, and reflective panels help visibility."]},{"h":"Loading and balance","p":["Distribute weight evenly side to side and keep it low. A heavily loaded rear rack changes handling, so brake earlier and take corners more slowly until you are used to it."]},{"h":"Securing the load","p":["Use straps or nets rated for the load, and check them regularly on a long ride. Make sure nothing can fall into the spokes or touch the wheel."]},{"h":"Choosing a rear rack for an e-bike","p":["Check the rack’s load rating, the bike’s own maximum load and whether the frame has the right mounting points. Racks that bolt to eyelets are more secure than clamp-on racks. Remember that the rider’s weight and the load both count towards the bike’s limit, and a heavy battery already uses part of it."]},{"h":"Carrying an e-bike by car","p":["E-bikes are heavy, so a car rack must be rated for the bike’s weight. Remove the battery before lifting, use a rack with a ramp or a tray if you can, and secure the bike with straps in several places. Check the rack, bike and number plate are visible and legal for your car, and never leave a lithium battery inside a hot car."]},{"h":"Baskets, panniers and bags compared","p":["Baskets are convenient for small items you want to reach quickly, but they raise the centre of gravity and can affect steering when loaded at the front. Panniers keep weight low and balanced either side of the rear wheel, and many are waterproof and easy to remove. Bags that strap to the frame or handlebar suit light loads and long rides. Choose based on what you carry most often, and keep heavy items low."]},{"h":"Trailers and when they make sense","ul":["A trailer suits bulky loads such as camping gear or shopping for a family.","Check the trailer’s load rating and the bike’s towing limit.","Fit a flag or lights so other road users can see the trailer.","Learn how it affects braking and turning in a quiet area first.","Check your state’s rules for trailers on bike paths."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How much can a rear rack carry?** Check the rack’s and the bike’s load ratings.","**Can I carry a child on a rack?** Only with a seat made for the rack and the bike.","**Do I need a special car rack for an e-bike?** Yes, one rated for the weight.","**Can I leave the battery on when carrying?** Remove it where you can, to lighten the bike and protect it."]},{"h":"Browse racks, bags and trailers","p":["See the [racks, bags and trailers](/shop/racks-bags-and-trailers/) category, and read the [cargo e-bike guide](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/) if you want a bike built for loads."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Can I put a rack on any e-bike?","a":"Most e-bikes can take a rack if the frame has mounting points or you use a compatible clamp. Check the rack’s maximum load against the bike’s limit and make sure it clears the brakes and battery. See the racks, bags & trailers range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do e-bikes need a special car rack?","a":"Yes, check the carrier’s weight limit. E-bikes are heavier than normal bikes, so choose a rack rated for the weight and remove the battery before travel where possible. Browse the racks, bags & trailers category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What racks and bags does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The racks, bags and trailers category lists rear racks, panniers, baskets, crates, trailers and a towbar carrier. Check each page for fit and load limits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a trailer better than panniers?","a":"Panniers keep the bike narrow and agile. A trailer carries more and bulkier loads but needs more space and care when turning. Choose based on how much you carry and where you ride. Browse the racks, bags & trailers category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike racks and carriers?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike racks and carriers in the racks, bags & trailers category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"basket for cycle","supporting":["bike with bags","bike with a trailer","rear cycle basket","cycle with basket","4 bike carrier"]},"words":642},"how-to-lock-an-e-bike":{"hook":"An e-bike needs a strong lock because it is worth more than a standard bicycle. A hardened chain or folding lock, used with a tracker, deters most opportunistic theft.","intro":["E-bikes are valuable and attractive to thieves, so a good lock matters more than on a normal bike. No lock is unbreakable, but a strong lock used well makes your bike a harder target.","This guide covers lock types, how to lock your bike and extra protection such as alarms and trackers."],"sections":[{"h":"Lock types","ul":["**D-locks** are compact and strong, and good for the frame and a wheel.","**Chain locks** are flexible and can secure the bike to a fixed object.","**Folding locks** pack small and give a good balance of strength and portability."]},{"h":"How to lock your bike","p":["Lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed object that cannot be lifted or cut, such as a purpose-built bike rail. Keep the lock off the ground, and fill as much of the lock’s space as you can so a thief has less room for tools. Use two different locks if you leave the bike for a long time."]},{"h":"Protect the battery and accessories","p":["Take a removable battery, display and lights with you, or use a lock for the battery if the bike has one. Parts are often stolen separately."]},{"h":"Alarms and trackers","p":["An alarm adds a deterrent and an alert, but it will not stop a determined thief. A tracker can help recover a stolen bike, and works best alongside a strong lock. Record the serial number, take photos of your bike and check your insurance."]},{"h":"Where to park","p":["Park in a busy, well-lit place with a purpose-built rail or a fixed object that cannot be lifted or cut. Avoid leaving a bike in the same place overnight where you can, and bring it inside if possible."]},{"h":"What to do if your bike is stolen","ul":["Report it to the police with the serial number and photos.","Tell your insurer.","Check online marketplaces and local bike shops.","Register the bike with a recovery service if one is available."]},{"h":"Before theft happens","p":["Record the serial number, take clear photos of the bike and any marks, and keep the receipt. Consider an engraving or marking kit, and a tracker if the bike is high-value."]},{"h":"Using two locks","p":["A single lock can be defeated, but two different lock types make a thief’s job harder and slower. A D-lock through the frame and rear wheel plus a cable or chain through the front wheel is a common combination. Choose locks of a good grade, and keep them off the ground so they cannot be hit with a hammer against the pavement."]},{"h":"Secure parking at home and work","ul":["Park the bike inside, out of sight, if you can.","Fit a ground anchor in your garage or shed.","Use a lock even for short stops.","Photograph the bike and record its serial number.","Check your home and contents insurance for e-bike cover."]},{"h":"Choosing a lock: what the ratings mean","p":["Many locks carry a security rating from an independent scheme. Higher ratings mean the lock resisted a wider range of tools for longer. A lock rated for high-risk areas is heavier and more expensive, but if your bike is worth thousands of dollars, the cost is small compared with the loss. Choose a lock whose rating matches the value of your bike and where you leave it.","Remember that a lock is only as good as what you attach it to. A thin signpost can be lifted off, and a flimsy rack can be cut. Choose a solid, fixed object that cannot be removed."]},{"h":"Reducing the risk at home","ul":["Keep the bike out of sight, in a locked garage or shed if possible.","Use a ground anchor and a good lock even inside.","Do not leave the battery on the bike if it is parked outside.","Check your insurance and record your serial number.","Consider a tracker for high-value bikes."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**What lock is best?** A well-rated D-lock or heavy chain, used with a second lock.","**Is a tracker worth it?** For valuable bikes, it can help recovery.","**Should I take the battery?** Yes, if it is removable.","**Does insurance cover theft?** Check your policy."]},{"h":"Browse locks","p":["See the [locks and security](/shop/locks-and-security/) category and compare lock types before you buy. For the full commuting setup, read our [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the best way to lock an e-bike?","a":"Lock the frame and a wheel to a fixed object using a hardened chain or D-lock, and keep the lock off the ground. Remove the battery or display if you can, and avoid leaving the bike in the same unsecured spot every day."},{"q":"Do bike trackers work on e-bikes?","a":"Trackers and tag mounts can help you find a stolen bike, but they do not stop theft. Hide the tracker well, register it, and combine it with a strong lock rather than relying on it alone. See the locks & security range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What locks does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The locks and security category lists chain locks, folding locks with alarms and a tracker mount. Each page states the lock type and size. Open the locks & security category to see everything that is currently in stock. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Is an alarmed lock worth it?","a":"An alarm adds a deterrent and an alert, but it will not stop a determined thief. It works best on top of a strong physical lock and good parking habits. See the locks & security range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list bike locks?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists bike locks in the locks & security category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"how to lock an e-bike","supporting":["how to lock an e-bike guide","how to lock an e-bike explained","how to lock an e-bike for beginners","how to lock an e-bike tips","how to lock an e-bike basics"]},"words":649},"chain-alignment-high-torque-motors":{"hook":"E-bike and electric dirt bike chains and sprockets wear faster under motor torque. Keep the chain clean, lightly lubricated and correctly tensioned, and replace worn sprockets as a set.","intro":["Motor torque stresses a chain and sprockets far more than pedalling does, so e-bike and electric dirt bike drivetrains wear faster. A clean, correctly tensioned and aligned chain lasts longer and runs quieter.","This guide covers cleaning, lubrication, tension and alignment, and when to replace worn parts."],"sections":[{"h":"Why chains wear faster on motorised bikes","p":["Higher torque and heavier loads stretch chains and wear sprocket teeth. A chain that is dirty, dry or misaligned wears faster still. Regular care costs far less than replacing a chain and sprockets early."]},{"h":"Cleaning and lubricating","p":["Clean the chain with a suitable degreaser and brush, rinse and dry it, then apply a lubricant made for chains. Wipe off the excess so it does not attract dirt. Clean and re-lubricate after wet or dusty rides. See the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category."]},{"h":"Chain tension and alignment","p":["A chain that is too tight stresses the bearings, and one that is too loose can jump off the sprocket or skip. Check tension at the middle of the chain run, as the manufacturer specifies, and make sure the front and rear sprockets are in line. Misalignment wears the chain and sprockets quickly."]},{"h":"When to replace the chain and sprockets","p":["Replace the chain when it stretches past the wear limit or skips under load, and change worn sprockets with it. Fitting a new chain on worn sprockets wears both out quickly, so replace them as a set."]},{"h":"Checking chain wear","p":["A chain wear gauge shows when a chain has stretched past its limit. For a quick check, lift the chain from the front chainring: if you can see a lot of the tooth through the gap, the chain or chainring is worn. Replace a stretched chain promptly to protect the sprockets."]},{"h":"Common drivetrain problems","ul":["Chain skipping under load: worn chain or sprockets.","Noise: dry or dirty chain.","Chain dropping off: misalignment or loose tension.","Rapid wear: dirt, poor lubrication or incorrect tension."]},{"h":"Cleaning without damaging the motor or battery","p":["Avoid high-pressure water around the motor, battery and electrical connectors. Use a damp cloth and a soft brush, and let the bike dry before you charge it."]},{"h":"A monthly drivetrain routine","ul":["Wipe the chain and check for dirt and wear.","Clean and lubricate the chain, then wipe off excess.","Check chain tension and sprocket alignment.","Inspect sprocket teeth for hooking or sharp edges.","Check that axle and sprocket bolts are tight."]},{"h":"Choosing the right chain lube","p":["Wet lube sticks to the chain and lasts longer in rain, but picks up dirt. Dry lube stays cleaner in dust, but needs reapplying more often. Pick one made for chains, apply a thin coat and wipe off the excess. Keep lubricant away from brake rotors and pads."]},{"h":"Setting chain tension and alignment by eye and by measurement","p":["On bikes with a rear derailleur, a tensioner keeps the chain taut. On single-speed or motorbike-style drives, tension is set by moving the rear wheel. Aim for a small amount of up-and-down play in the middle of the chain run, as specified by the maker. Too tight stresses bearings, and too loose lets the chain jump.","To check alignment, look along the chain from the rear and see whether the chain runs in a straight line between the front and rear sprockets. A straight edge held against the sprockets can help. Make small adjustments and recheck after tightening the axle."]},{"h":"Signs that you need a new chain or sprockets","ul":["The chain skips under load.","Teeth are hooked or shark-fin shaped.","The chain has stretched past the wear limit.","You hear grinding or crunching that does not clear after cleaning.","Adjustment range has run out."]},{"h":"Parts and related guides","p":["Browse chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts in the [motors, drivetrain and sprockets](/shop/motors-drivetrain-and-sprockets/) category. Read about [conversion kits](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and [Sur-Ron spares](/shop/sur-ron-spares/) if you are upgrading."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How often should I lubricate an e-bike chain?","a":"Clean and lubricate the chain regularly, and after wet or dusty rides. Wipe off excess lube so it does not attract dirt. Motor torque stresses the chain, so check wear and tension often. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"When should I replace the chain and sprockets?","a":"Replace the chain when it stretches past the maker’s wear limit or skips under load, and change worn sprockets with it. Fitting a new chain on worn sprockets wears both out quickly. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What drivetrain parts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists drive chains, sprockets, bottom bracket assemblies, motors and torque arms. The care category lists degreasers and rotor cleaners. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a belt drive better than a chain?","a":"Belts are quiet and clean but need a compatible frame and cost more. Chains are cheaper, easier to find and simple to repair. Most motorised bikes use chains, so check your bike’s drivetrain before choosing. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists chains, sprockets and drivetrain parts in the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"cycle sprocket","supporting":["sprocket in bike","new bike chain","ebike maintenance","cycle sprocket guide","how to choose cycle sprocket"]},"words":569},"pedal-assist-vs-throttle":{"hook":"A pedal-assist e-bike adds motor power as you pedal, while a throttle e-bike adds power when you twist or press a throttle. Some bikes offer both.","intro":["A pedal-assist e-bike adds motor power as you pedal, while a throttle e-bike adds power when you twist or press a throttle. Some bikes offer both.","The difference affects how the bike feels, how far it goes and how Australian rules treat it."],"sections":[{"h":"How pedal assist works","p":["Sensors detect that you are pedalling, and the motor adds power in proportion to the assist level you choose. The bike feels like a bicycle with a helping hand, and battery range is usually longer than with throttle use."]},{"h":"How a throttle works","p":["A throttle gives power on demand without pedalling. It is convenient for starting from a standstill and for riders who cannot pedal continuously. A throttle uses battery faster, and it changes how the bike is classed."]},{"h":"Which is road legal?","p":["In Australia a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W standard can use public roads, subject to state rules. A throttle that drives the motor at higher speeds changes how the bike is treated. Check your state’s road authority and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Adding a throttle to a bike","p":["Some controllers accept a throttle and some do not, and adding one can change the bike’s classification. Check compatibility with your controller and your state’s rules first. See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category."]},{"h":"Cadence sensors and torque sensors","p":["Pedal-assist systems use one of two sensors. A cadence sensor detects that you are pedalling and gives a set amount of assist, which feels on and off. A torque sensor measures how hard you pedal and scales the assist to match, which feels more natural and responsive. Torque-sensor bikes usually cost more."]},{"h":"Choosing by how you ride","ul":["Commuting with frequent stops: a throttle or a responsive pedal-assist system helps you start.","Fitness and longer rides: pedal assist keeps you working and stretches range.","Off-road riding: throttle control is common on dirt bikes."]},{"h":"Display, assist levels and range","p":["Assist levels trade range for effort. Use a lower level on flat ground and a higher one on hills. Check the display for battery and assist information, and learn how the bike behaves in each level before you rely on it."]},{"h":"Examples of when each system makes sense","ul":["A rider who wants fitness and range: pedal assist.","A rider with a bad knee who needs a boost at start-up: pedal assist with walk-assist, or a throttle.","A rider who rides a lot in stop-start traffic: a throttle or a responsive torque sensor.","An off-road rider on private land: throttle control is typical."]},{"h":"What to check in the product description","p":["Look for the words “pedal assist”, “PAS”, “throttle” or “twist throttle”, and whether the throttle works only up to a set speed or at any speed. Check the maximum assist speed and the rated motor power. If a seller does not state them, ask before you buy."]},{"h":"Understanding assist levels and modes","p":["Pedal-assist bikes usually offer several levels, from a gentle boost to maximum help. A low level stretches the battery and keeps you working, while a high level helps on hills and with loads. Some bikes also offer a walk mode that moves the bike slowly while you walk beside it, which is useful on stairs and ramps. Learn what each level does on a quiet road before you rely on it in traffic.","On a bike with a throttle, check whether the throttle works at all speeds or only at low speed, and whether it needs you to pedal. These details change how the bike feels and how it is treated by the rules."]},{"h":"Choosing by comfort and fitness goals","ul":["If you want exercise, use pedal assist at lower levels.","If you want to arrive fresh, use higher assist levels.","If you have health concerns, ask your doctor about the right level of effort.","If you ride with others, choose a level that lets you keep pace comfortably."]},{"h":"Which should you choose?","p":["A throttle is convenient, while pedal assist feels more like cycling and usually stretches range. For commuting see the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/), and for how to pick a bike overall see [how to choose an e-bike](/blog/how-to-choose-an-e-bike/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between pedal assist and a throttle?","a":"Pedal assist adds motor power only while you pedal, and the assist level is set on the display. A throttle gives power on demand without pedalling. Rules about throttles vary by bike type and state. See the controls, grips & pedals range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I add a throttle to a pedal-assist e-bike?","a":"Some controllers accept a throttle, but not all, and adding one can change how the bike is classed. Check compatibility with your controller and your state’s rules before fitting one. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What throttles and displays does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists twist and thumb throttles, displays and switches. Each page states the models it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a throttle better than pedal assist?","a":"A throttle is convenient and good for starting from a standstill, while pedal assist feels more like cycling and usually stretches range. The best choice depends on how you ride and which rules apply to your bike. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list throttles and controls?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists throttles and controls in the controls, grips & pedals category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"throttle bike","supporting":["throttle of bike","throttle bike guide","how to choose throttle bike","throttle bike explained","throttle bike for beginners"]},"words":623},"commuter-e-bikes":{"hook":"A commuter e-bike is a pedal-assist bike built for daily city riding, with mudguards, lights and a rack. Step-through frames make mounting easy.","intro":["A commuter e-bike is built for daily city riding. It usually has an upright or step-through frame, mudguards, a rear rack and lights, and a pedal-assist motor that makes hills and headwinds easier.","This guide covers what to look for and how to set the bike up for the daily ride."],"sections":[{"h":"Frame style and fit","p":["Step-through frames are easy to get on and off, and upright frames suit most riders. Choose a frame size that matches your height, and test the reach to the handlebars for comfort over longer rides."]},{"h":"Range and battery for your route","p":["Measure your round trip, and add a margin for hills and weather. Charge the battery at home or at work. Larger batteries add range and weight, so choose the capacity that suits your distance."]},{"h":"Practical features","ul":["Mudguards keep you clean in the wet.","A rear rack carries a bag or panniers.","Integrated lights help you be seen: read the [e-bike lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/).","Reliable brakes matter more on a heavier bike."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike cheaper than commuting by car?","p":["For short trips, an e-bike can cost much less to run because electricity costs are low and there is no fuel or parking cost, but you still pay for the bike, servicing and a good lock. Work out your own numbers before deciding."]},{"h":"Weather, clothing and comfort","p":["Mudguards, a chain guard and a rain jacket keep you clean and dry. Choose gloves for cold mornings and ventilated clothing for hot days, and carry spare layers in a pannier. A comfortable saddle and grips make long commutes easier."]},{"h":"Charging at work and at home","p":["A removable battery is easier to charge indoors. Charge in a ventilated area, and do not leave it charging unattended. Ask your workplace if there are secure bike parking and charging options."]},{"h":"Servicing a commuter bike","p":["Daily use wears tyres, brake pads and the chain. Check tyre pressure weekly, and have the brakes and drivetrain checked regularly. Keep the battery contacts clean and dry."]},{"h":"Setting up a commuter bike properly","ul":["Fit mudguards and a chain guard to keep clothes clean.","Add a rear rack and panniers or a bag.","Fit front and rear lights and a bell.","Choose puncture-resistant tyres.","Carry a small repair kit and a pump."]},{"h":"Planning your route and your charge","p":["Check that bike paths and roads on your route allow e-bikes, plan for hills and headwinds, and start with a full charge. If your trip is close to your battery’s range, charge at work or choose a larger battery. Check your state’s rules for helmets and lights before you set off."]},{"h":"A sample commute: what to pack and how to ride","p":["Imagine a 12 km ride to work with a couple of hills. You would pack a small repair kit, a rain jacket, a lock and a change of clothes, set the assist to a medium level on the flat and higher on hills, and plan to arrive with spare charge. Keep your lights on in low light, use a bell on shared paths, and leave extra room when you brake, because e-bikes weigh more and take longer to stop.","In the first few weeks, note how much battery you use each day. That tells you whether your battery is big enough, and whether you can ride without charging at work. Over time, you will learn to choose the assist level that gets you there comfortably without draining the battery."]},{"h":"Common commuting problems and fixes","ul":["**Punctures:** use puncture-protected tyres and carry a spare tube.","**Sweaty arrival:** use a lower assist on flat ground and a higher one on hills, and wear breathable clothes.","**Theft worries:** lock the frame and wheel to a fixed rail, and take the battery with you.","**Wet weather:** fit mudguards, wear a waterproof jacket and brake earlier.","**Battery range anxiety:** choose a bigger battery or charge at work."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Do I need to pedal?** On a pedal-assist e-bike, yes, but the motor helps.","**Can I ride in the rain?** Yes, with mudguards and care, and avoid pressure washing electrical parts.","**How do I stop the bike being stolen?** Use a strong lock, park in busy places and take the battery.","**How fast can I go?** On a road-legal e-bike, assistance stops at about 25 km/h, and you can pedal faster than that on your own."]},{"h":"Rules, locks and gear","p":["Pedal-assist e-bikes at 250W can use roads and paths, subject to state rules: see [e-bike laws in Australia](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Wear an approved helmet, and read the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/). Browse [commuter and urban e-bikes](/shop/commuter-and-urban-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is a step-through e-bike?","a":"A step-through e-bike has a low frame section so you can mount without lifting your leg over a top tube. It suits riders who want easy mounting, carry loads or ride in everyday clothes. See the commuter & urban e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How far can I commute on an e-bike?","a":"It depends on the battery, rider weight, terrain and assist level, so check the stated range and allow a margin. Most daily commutes sit well within a typical battery, and charging at work or home resets the next trip. Browse the commuter & urban e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which commuter e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The commuter and urban e-bikes category lists step-through, hybrid and city models from several brands. Open a product to see its motor, battery and price. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an e-bike cheaper than commuting by car?","a":"For short trips, an e-bike can cost much less to run because electricity costs are low and there is no fuel or parking. Compare your own trip distances and the purchase price to work out the break-even point. Browse the commuter & urban e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list commuter e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists commuter e-bikes in the commuter & urban e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"retro cruiser electric bike","supporting":["retro cruiser electric bike guide","how to choose retro cruiser electric bike","retro cruiser electric bike explained","retro cruiser electric bike for beginners","retro cruiser electric bike tips"]},"words":690},"best-phone-mounts-for-e-bikes":{"hook":"A good bike phone mount holds the phone firmly over rough surfaces. Look for a secure lock, a handlebar clamp that fits your bars and a design that reduces vibration.","intro":["A phone mount lets you use maps and ride data without holding your phone. It needs to hold the phone securely over bumps, fit your handlebar and be easy to remove.","This guide covers the main mount types and what to check."],"sections":[{"h":"Clamp mounts and case-based mounts","p":["A clamp mount grips the edges of any phone and works across phones. A case-based system holds the phone more securely and removes quickly, but needs a compatible case. Choose by how often you change phones and how rough your rides are."]},{"h":"Handlebar fit","p":["Check the mount’s clamp size against your handlebar diameter, and make sure it does not clash with brake levers, lights or a display. A stem mount can be a good alternative if the bar is crowded."]},{"h":"Vibration and your phone camera","p":["Motor and road vibration can damage some phone camera stabilisation systems. Consider a mount with vibration damping if you ride on rough surfaces often."]},{"h":"Weather and security","p":["A waterproof case or cover protects the phone in rain. Remove the phone when you park, and secure the mount so it cannot be taken easily."]},{"h":"Using navigation safely","p":["Set your route before you start, and use voice directions where you can. Glance at the screen only when it is safe, and keep your eyes on the road. Check that the mount does not block your view or the display on the bike."]},{"h":"Power and charging","p":["Navigation drains a phone battery quickly. Some mounts include wireless or USB charging, and a small power bank can keep the phone going on a long ride. Protect charging ports from rain."]},{"h":"Mirrors and audio accessories","p":["A handlebar mirror improves awareness behind you, and a small speaker or bone-conduction headphones can add audio without blocking traffic noise. Check your state’s rules on headphones while riding. See the [phone mounts, mirrors and audio](/shop/phone-mounts-mirrors-and-audio/) category."]},{"h":"Choosing between mount styles","ul":["Clamp mounts fit most phones and are easy to swap.","Case-based mounts are more secure and quick to remove.","Stem mounts keep the handlebar uncluttered.","Bar-end and top-cap mounts suit specific setups."]},{"h":"Keeping your phone safe","p":["Use a mount with a secure lock, add a strap or tether if you ride rough ground, and remove the phone when you park. Keep an eye on temperature, because phones can overheat in direct sun while charging."]},{"h":"Choosing a mount that suits your handlebar","p":["Handlebars come in several diameters, and e-bikes often have extra fittings such as displays, bells and light mounts that take up space. Before you buy a mount, measure your bar and look at where it will sit. Many mounts use rubber shims to fit different diameters, and some clamp to the stem instead, which can be cleaner on a busy cockpit.","Check that the mount does not block your view of the display or your reach for the brakes and shifters. Fit it where you can see the phone with a glance down, not a long stare."]},{"h":"Protecting your phone from vibration and weather","ul":["Use a mount with some vibration damping, especially on rough surfaces.","Use a waterproof case or cover in the rain.","Keep the phone out of direct sun when charging, to avoid overheating.","Take the phone with you when you park the bike.","Check that the mount grips firmly before you ride, and recheck after the first few rides."]},{"h":"Using your phone safely while riding","p":["A phone mount is for navigation and ride data, not for texting or calling. Set up your route before you leave, use voice prompts if you can, and pull over in a safe place if you need to change anything. Check your state’s rules about using phones while riding."]},{"h":"Browse mounts","p":["See the [phone mounts, mirrors and audio](/shop/phone-mounts-mirrors-and-audio/) category, and for the full commuting setup read the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I stop vibration damaging my phone camera?","a":"Strong vibration can affect some phone cameras. Use a mount with a vibration damper or a stem or bar-end position that sees less shake, and remove the phone on very rough ground. See the phone mounts, mirrors & audio range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Will a phone mount fit my handlebars?","a":"Most clamps fit common handlebar diameters, but check the range on the product page. Thick grips, cables or controls can block placement, so measure your bars first. Browse the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What phone mounts does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The phone mounts, mirrors and audio category lists handlebar mounts, out-front mounts and phone cases from several brands. Each page states the phone and handlebar fit. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a case-based mount better than a clamp?","a":"A case-based system holds the phone more securely and removes quickly, but it needs a compatible case. A universal clamp fits more phones but can be less secure on rough ground. Browse the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list phone mounts?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists phone mounts in the phone mounts, mirrors & audio category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"phone mounts for e-bikes guide","supporting":["phone mounts for e-bikes guide","how to choose phone mounts for e-bikes","phone mounts for e-bikes explained","phone mounts for e-bikes tips","phone mounts for e-bikes basics"]},"words":576},"how-to-choose-an-e-bike":{"hook":"Choosing an e-bike comes down to where you ride, how much power you need and how far you go. Motor power, battery capacity and frame size matter most.","intro":["Choosing an e-bike comes down to six decisions: where you ride, how much motor power you need, how far you need to go, what size and style of bike fits you, what brakes and parts you want, and what you can spend. Answer them in that order and the shortlist makes itself.","This guide walks through each step in plain language, with the questions to ask before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"Step 1: Where will you ride?","p":["Write down your three most common trips. A flat ride to the station, a hilly commute, a weekend trail and a trip with shopping each point to a different bike.","Roads and bike paths point to a pedal-assist e-bike that meets the 250W rule. Private land and rough trails point to a mountain bike or an off-road bike. See our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you decide."]},{"h":"Step 2: Motor power and how it is delivered","p":["On a road-legal e-bike the motor is limited to 250W, so choose on quality and feel rather than watts. Mid-drive motors give natural power on hills, and hub motors are simple and common on lower-priced bikes. For off-road bikes, higher power means more speed and more risk, so match it to the rider. Our [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/) explains how power is delivered."]},{"h":"Step 3: Battery capacity and range","p":["A larger battery gives more range but adds weight and cost. Quoted range is a best case: hills, wind, heavy riders and high assist levels shorten it. Choose a battery with margin for your longest regular trip, and read the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) so it lasts.","If you want long range, look at models with larger batteries or a removable second battery."]},{"h":"Step 4: Frame style and size","ul":["Step-through frames are easy to mount and suit mixed-ability riders.","Folding frames suit small spaces: see [folding e-bikes](/blog/folding-electric-bikes/).","Cargo frames carry loads: see [electric cargo bikes](/blog/electric-cargo-bikes/).","Mountain and fat-tyre frames suit rough ground: see [electric mountain bikes](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) and [fat-tyre e-bikes](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."],"p":["Check the maker’s size chart against your height and inside leg, because an incorrect size spoils the ride however good the bike is."]},{"h":"Step 5: Brakes, tyres and components","p":["Heavier, faster bikes need good brakes: look for hydraulic disc brakes on hilly or fast bikes, and see the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/). Check tyre width for your surfaces, and make sure lights, mudguards and a rack are included if you commute."]},{"h":"Step 6: Budget, warranty and support","p":["Set a budget, and compare bikes of the same type in the same price band. The [budget e-bikes](/electric-bikes/budget/) page lists the lowest-priced models. Look at the warranty, who handles claims and how easy it is to get parts. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help comparing two bikes.","To see every type in one place, browse [all electric bikes](/electric-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Common mistakes when choosing an e-bike","ul":["Buying for the best-case range and not the real one.","Choosing the wrong frame size because the price was good.","Ignoring brakes, which matter more on a heavier bike.","Assuming a powerful bike is road legal.","Forgetting about charging, storage and weight."]},{"h":"Test, compare and ask","p":["If you can, test ride bikes of the type you are considering, and compare two or three in the same price band. Ask the seller about warranty, parts and servicing, and check how you will get help if something goes wrong. If you buy online, ask about returns and delivery before you order."]},{"h":"Buying for another person","p":["If you are buying for someone else, ask about their height, strength and riding experience, and check the rules for where they will ride. For a gift, a bike with a lower step-through frame, good brakes and a modest motor is often the safest choice."]},{"h":"After you buy","p":["Read the manual, check the bolts and brakes, set up the saddle and handlebars, and charge the battery as the maker advises. Wear a helmet, learn the controls in a quiet place and service the bike regularly."]},{"h":"Match the bike to your life with five quick scenarios","ul":["**Daily commute of 10 km, mostly flat:** a commuter e-bike with a mid-size battery, mudguards, a rack and lights.","**Hilly commute of 20 km:** a commuter or trekking bike with a larger battery and a motor with good climbing torque.","**Weekend trail riding:** an e-MTB, hardtail or full suspension depending on budget and trail roughness.","**Small flat and a train commute:** a folding e-bike you can carry and store easily.","**Kids, shopping and errands:** a cargo e-bike or trike with a high load rating and strong brakes."]},{"h":"How to test fit and comfort","p":["A good fit makes a bigger difference than any component. Stand over the frame with your feet flat: you should have clearance. Sit on the saddle with a slight bend in your knee at the bottom of the pedal stroke. Reach for the handlebars with a slight bend in your elbows and no strain in your shoulders. If anything feels stretched or cramped, try a different frame size or ask about adjustable stems and seat posts.","Test the controls too. You should be able to reach the brake levers and display without letting go of the bars, and the controls should be easy to use with gloves on."]},{"h":"Costs beyond the price tag","ul":["A helmet, lights, a lock and possibly panniers or a rack.","Servicing: brake pads, tyres, chains and eventually a battery.","Insurance: check whether your policy covers an e-bike and what it excludes.","Charging: electricity is cheap, but you need a safe place to charge.","Delivery: freight for a heavy bike with a lithium battery costs more than for a normal bike."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller","ul":["What is the motor’s continuous power, and does the bike meet the pedal-assist definition in my state?","What is the battery’s capacity in watt-hours, and what range do riders actually get?","What does the warranty cover, for how long and who handles a claim?","Are spare batteries, chargers and brake pads available separately?","What are the delivery arrangements for a lithium battery?"]},{"h":"Quick answers from first-time buyers","ul":["**How much range do I need?** Add up your longest regular trip, then add a margin of about 30 to 50 per cent.","**Should I choose a mid-drive or a hub motor?** Mid-drives feel natural on hills, and hubs are simpler and cheaper.","**Can I fit a child seat?** Check the frame, the rack and the maker’s guidance, and use only seats made for the bike.","**How do I transport it?** Check the bike’s weight and the rack’s rating, and remove the battery before lifting.","**What if I do not like it?** Check the return policy before you order, especially for online purchases."]},{"h":"A final decision checklist","ul":["I know where and how I will ride, and the rules there.","I know my budget, including accessories.","The frame size fits me.","The battery is big enough for my longest trip.","The brakes suit the bike’s weight.","I know who will service and support the bike."]},{"h":"A quick checklist before you buy","ul":["Does it meet the rules for where I will ride?","Is the battery big enough for my longest trip?","Does the frame size suit my height?","Are the brakes strong enough for the bike’s weight?","Is there warranty and parts support in Australia?"]}],"faq":[{"q":"How fast does a 250W e-bike go?","a":"In Australia a road-legal 250W pedal-assist e-bike’s motor assistance cuts out at 25 km/h, though you can ride faster by pedalling. Speed on the flat also depends on rider weight, terrain and wind. See the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How much motor power do I need?","a":"A 250W motor suits road and path riding and meets the Australian standard for pedal-assist e-bikes. Higher-power motors are for off-road and private property use only. Choose by where you will ride. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Can Torque E-Bikes help me choose?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp with your riding plans and budget and they will suggest models from the range. Each category page also lists the models with prices. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a bigger battery always better?","a":"A bigger battery adds range but also weight and cost. If your trips are short, a smaller battery may be enough. Match capacity to your normal trip with some margin. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes for every type of rider?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes for every type of rider in the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"long range ebikes","supporting":["how fast does an electric bicycle go","how fast do electric bikes go","how fast can an e bike go","electric bicycle range","how fast does a n ebike go"]},"words":1113},"what-is-an-e-bike":{"hook":"An e-bike is a bicycle with an electric motor and rechargeable battery that assists the rider. Most e-bikes add power as you pedal, and some also have a throttle.","intro":["An e-bike is a bicycle with an electric motor and battery that helps you pedal. You still ride the bike, but the motor adds power so hills, headwinds and longer trips take less effort.","This guide explains how an e-bike works, the main types and how it differs from an electric motorbike."],"sections":[{"h":"How an e-bike works","p":["A battery supplies power to a motor, and a controller manages how much power the motor gives. On a pedal-assist e-bike, a sensor detects pedalling and the controller adds power in proportion to the assist level you choose. The bike has the same brakes, gears and tyres as a normal bike, but built for the extra weight and speed."]},{"h":"The main parts","ul":["The motor: in the front or rear wheel (hub) or at the cranks (mid-drive).","The battery: a rechargeable lithium pack, usually on the frame or rack.","The controller and display: manage power and show assist level and battery.","The sensor: reads pedalling (cadence or torque)."]},{"h":"Types of e-bike","p":["Commuter, mountain, folding, cargo, fat-tyre and road e-bikes all use the same basic parts. See the main types in our [e-bike buying guide](/blog/best-electric-bikes-in-australia/), or browse every category on the [electric bikes](/electric-bikes/) page."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike better than a normal bike?","p":["An e-bike makes hills and longer trips easier and suits riders who want less effort. A normal bike is lighter and cheaper to own. If distance or hills put you off riding, an e-bike usually pays for itself in use."]},{"h":"How far can an e-bike go?","p":["Range depends on battery capacity, assist level, rider weight, terrain, wind, tyre pressure and temperature. A typical pedal-assist e-bike can cover many kilometres on a charge, but the maker’s figure is a best case. Choose a battery with margin for your longest trip."]},{"h":"Do you still need to pedal?","p":["On a pedal-assist e-bike, yes: the motor helps while you pedal. Some bikes also have a throttle, which gives power without pedalling. See the [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/)."]},{"h":"Charging and cost to run","p":["An e-bike battery charges from a normal power point, and a full charge uses a small amount of electricity. Running costs are low compared with a car, though you still pay for servicing and replacement parts such as tyres, brake pads and eventually a battery."]},{"h":"Common e-bike myths","ul":["“E-bikes do all the work.” On a pedal-assist bike you still pedal.","“E-bikes are only for older riders.” Riders of every age use them for commuting, trails and errands.","“E-bikes can go anywhere.” Rules and trail access still apply.","“The battery is the only thing to worry about.” Brakes, tyres and the drivetrain also wear faster on a heavier bike."]},{"h":"What you need to get started","ul":["A helmet that meets the standard.","A lock and lights.","A safe place to charge.","A bit of practice in a quiet area."]},{"h":"The different kinds of e-bike","ul":["**City and commuter bikes** with upright positions, mudguards and racks.","**Mountain bikes** with suspension and strong brakes.","**Folding bikes** for small spaces and transport.","**Cargo bikes and trikes** for loads and passengers.","**Fat-tyre bikes** for sand, gravel and snow.","**Road and gravel bikes** with a light motor."]},{"h":"Is an e-bike right for you?","p":["An e-bike suits you if hills, distance or headwinds put you off riding, if you want to commute without arriving sweaty, or if you want to carry loads. It may not suit you if you only ride short, flat trips and prefer the lightest possible bike. If you are unsure, hire or test ride one first, and read our [guide to choosing an e-bike](/blog/how-to-choose-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How fast can an e-bike go?** On a road-legal e-bike, assistance stops at about 25 km/h.","**How long does a battery last on a ride?** It depends on the size, assist level, terrain and rider.","**Is an e-bike hard to maintain?** The bike needs the same care as a normal bike plus battery care.","**Do I need a licence?** Not for a road-legal pedal-assist e-bike, but check your state."]},{"h":"E-bike or electric motorbike?","p":["An e-bike is a bicycle with pedal assist. An electric motorbike, or e-moto, has a throttle and a much more powerful motor, and is for off-road use in Australia. Read [what an e-moto is](/blog/what-is-an-e-moto/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How does an e-bike work?","a":"A sensor detects pedalling or a throttle input, the controller draws power from the battery, and the motor adds drive to the wheel or cranks. The rider still steers, brakes and, on pedal-assist bikes, pedals. See the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How do I charge an e-bike?","a":"Plug the charger into the battery or the bike and a standard wall outlet. Use only the charger supplied for your battery, charge in a safe, ventilated spot and do not leave it charging unattended. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where can I see all the e-bike types?","a":"The electric bikes hub links to every category: commuter, mountain, folding, cargo, road and fat-tyre e-bikes. Pick a category to compare models and prices. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an e-bike better than a normal bike?","a":"An e-bike makes hills and longer trips easier and suits riders who want less effort. A normal bike is lighter and cheaper to own. It depends on how far, how hilly and how often you ride. Browse the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bikes for first-time riders?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bikes for first-time riders in the electric bikes (all e-bikes hub) category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"what is an ebike","supporting":["what is a ebike","what is an ebike guide","how to choose what is an ebike","what is an ebike explained","what is an ebike for beginners"]},"words":647},"fat-tyre-e-bikes":{"hook":"Fat-tyre e-bikes use wide tyres for grip and comfort on sand, gravel and rough ground. Scrambler styles add a moto-inspired frame and motor.","intro":["A fat-tyre e-bike uses very wide tyres for grip and comfort on sand, gravel, dirt and rough tracks. Add a motor and battery and you have a bike that is easy to ride on surfaces that stop a normal bike.","This guide covers what they suit, what to check and how they compare with an electric mountain bike."],"sections":[{"h":"What fat-tyre e-bikes are good for","ul":["Sand and beach tracks.","Gravel roads and unsealed tracks.","Relaxed riding with a comfortable, stable feel.","Scrambler-style bikes that pair wide tyres with a moto-inspired frame."]},{"h":"Fat-tyre or e-MTB?","p":["A fat-tyre bike suits sand, gravel and relaxed riding, while an e-MTB suits technical trails with more suspension and agile handling. Fat tyres add weight and rolling resistance on hard surfaces, which can shorten range. Read the [e-MTB buying guide](/blog/electric-mountain-bike-buying-guide/) to compare."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Tyre width and tread for your surfaces.","Motor type and power, and whether it is pedal assist.","Battery capacity for the range you want.","Brakes: heavier bikes with wide tyres need strong brakes.","Frame size and rider limit."]},{"h":"Rules for riding","p":["A bike with a motor above 250W is for off-road and private property use only. Check the product page and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) before you ride on public roads."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure and surfaces","p":["Fat tyres run at much lower pressures than normal tyres. Lower pressure gives more grip and float on sand and soft ground, and higher pressure rolls easier on hard surfaces. Use a gauge made for low pressures and follow the maker’s range. See the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Weight and handling","p":["Wide tyres and larger batteries add weight, so these bikes feel solid on rough ground but heavier to lift and store. Check the weight and the rider limit, and consider a stand or rack that suits a heavier bike."]},{"h":"Where fat-tyre bikes work well","p":["They suit beach tracks, gravel roads, fire trails and relaxed riding. Check local rules about riding on beaches and trails, because they vary and some areas restrict e-bikes."]},{"h":"Accessories that suit fat-tyre bikes","p":["Wide mudguards, a sturdy rack, a bright light and a good lock make a fat-tyre bike far more practical. Check that accessories are sized for the wide tyres and the frame, and confirm the rack’s load rating before you carry anything heavy."]},{"h":"Riding on sand and gravel safely","ul":["Let some air out of the tyres for soft sand and check pressure before you head home.","Brake early and gently, because wide tyres can slide.","Rinse salt off the bike and dry it after beach riding.","Check local rules before riding on beaches and trails."]},{"h":"Understanding the trade-offs of wide tyres","p":["Wide tyres give grip, comfort and flotation on soft ground. The price is weight, rolling resistance and sometimes a less precise feel on hard roads. On a fat-tyre e-bike the motor covers the extra effort, but the battery pays for it: expect shorter range than a slimmer-tyred e-bike with the same battery. If you ride mostly on sealed roads, a narrower tyre may suit you better."]},{"h":"Fitting and servicing fat tyres","ul":["Use tubes made for the tyre width and check the valve type.","Use a gauge that reads low pressures accurately.","Check that mudguards, racks and kickstands are rated for wide tyres.","Check the wheel bearings and spokes regularly, because heavier bikes stress them."]},{"h":"Who should and should not buy a fat-tyre e-bike","p":["A fat-tyre bike suits riders who ride on sand, gravel, farm tracks and soft ground, who value comfort and stability, and who do not mind extra weight. It suits less well if your riding is mostly fast sealed roads, if you need a light bike to carry upstairs or if storage space is tight. If you are unsure, compare it with a standard commuter or e-MTB in the same price band."]},{"h":"Accessories to add","ul":["Wide mudguards.","A rear rack rated for the load.","A strong lock.","Lights.","A tyre pressure gauge made for low pressures."]},{"h":"Models and brands to browse","p":["See the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, and the [Flight Risk](/blog/flight-risk-fr505-and-fr555/) and [DiroDi Rover](/blog/dirodi-rover-gen-6/) guides for popular models. Maintain tyres with the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are fat-tyre e-bikes good on sand?","a":"Wide, low-pressure tyres spread the load and give better grip on soft ground than narrow tyres. Check the tyre size and pressure guidance, and expect more rolling resistance on hard surfaces. See the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How heavy are fat-tyre e-bikes?","a":"They are heavier than standard e-bikes because of the larger tyres and stronger frames, so check the weight on each product page if you need to lift or carry the bike. Browse the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which fat-tyre e-bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes category lists models from Fatboy, Cruzr, DiroDi, Aventon and others, with prices on each product page. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is a fat-tyre e-bike better than a mountain e-bike?","a":"A fat-tyre bike suits sand, gravel and relaxed riding, while an e-MTB suits technical trails with suspension. Pick by the surface you ride on most. Browse the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category to compare models side by side. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes range."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list fat-tyre e-bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists fat-tyre e-bikes in the fat-tyre & scrambler e-bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"fat tyre mountain bike","supporting":["bolzzen super street","electric scrambler bike","bolzzen atom pro","cruzr stoke af","bolzzen phoenix"]},"words":621},"electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety":{"hook":"An e-bike battery is a rechargeable lithium pack rated in volts and amp-hours. Voltage affects power delivery and capacity affects range.","intro":["The battery is the most valuable and most safety-critical part of an e-bike or electric dirt bike. Looking after it extends its life, and following simple safety rules reduces fire risk.","This guide covers charging, storage, signs of trouble and how lithium batteries are shipped."],"sections":[{"h":"Charging","ul":["Use the charger supplied with the battery, or one the maker recommends.","Charge in a ventilated spot, away from flammable items, and do not leave it charging unattended or overnight.","Stop using a battery that is swollen, hot, leaking or damaged.","Unplug when the battery is full."]},{"h":"Storage and winter care","p":["Store the battery somewhere cool and dry, away from heat and direct sun. If the bike will sit for weeks, leave the battery partly charged rather than full or empty, and check it every month or two. Follow the maker’s manual for the recommended storage level."]},{"h":"Getting more life from a battery","p":["Avoid fully draining the battery regularly, avoid extreme heat and cold, and keep contacts clean and dry. A battery gradually loses capacity over its life, so range falls slowly even with good care."]},{"h":"Shipping lithium batteries","p":["Lithium batteries are Class 9 dangerous goods and travel only with carriers approved for dangerous goods freight. This is why delivery of a bike or battery needs extra planning, and why Torque E-Bikes confirms freight with you before payment."]},{"h":"Signs a battery needs attention","ul":["Swelling, a bulging case or a hot spot.","A smell of chemicals or smoke.","A sudden, large drop in range.","The battery not holding charge or cutting out under load."],"p":["If you see any of these, stop using the battery, keep it away from anything flammable and contact the maker or a specialist."]},{"h":"Charging habits that help","p":["Charge when convenient rather than letting the battery run flat. Avoid charging a hot battery straight after a hard ride, and let it cool first. In very cold weather, let the battery warm to room temperature before charging."]},{"h":"End of life and disposal","p":["When a battery no longer holds a useful charge, do not put it in the household bin. Take it to a battery recycling drop-off point, or ask the maker or a bike shop how to dispose of it safely."]},{"h":"Understanding volts, amp-hours and watt-hours","p":["Voltage tells you the pressure, amp-hours tell you the charge capacity, and watt-hours (volts multiplied by amp-hours) tell you the energy stored. A 48V 10Ah battery holds 480Wh. When you compare batteries, compare watt-hours, because voltage on its own does not tell you how long the bike will run."]},{"h":"Safe charging habits at home","ul":["Charge on a hard, non-flammable surface.","Keep the charger and battery away from curtains, beds and fuel.","Fit a smoke alarm near where you charge.","Do not charge a damaged battery.","Use the charger that came with the battery."]},{"h":"Winter, summer and storage temperature","p":["Batteries work best at moderate temperatures. In cold weather, range falls and charging can be slower, so bring the battery to room temperature before charging. In hot weather, avoid leaving the battery in a closed car or in direct sun, because heat shortens its life and increases risk. Store the battery indoors, away from heaters and out of direct sunlight."]},{"h":"What to do if a battery is damaged","ul":["Stop using it and do not charge it.","Move it outside, away from anything flammable, if it is hot or smoking.","Contact the maker or a battery specialist.","Do not try to open or repair the pack yourself.","Dispose of dead batteries through a recycling drop-off, not the household bin."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How long does an e-bike battery last?** Many last several years, but capacity falls gradually with use and age.","**Can I leave the battery on charge overnight?** Avoid it. Charge while you are awake and nearby, and unplug when full.","**Can I buy a battery from a different brand?** Only if the voltage, connector and communication system match your bike. Check with the seller.","**Can I fly with an e-bike battery?** Airlines restrict lithium batteries, so check the airline’s rules before you travel."]},{"h":"Buying a replacement","p":["Match the voltage, connector and size to your bike. See the [batteries, chargers and electronics](/shop/batteries-chargers-and-electronics/) category, and read the [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) if you are building a kit."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between 48V and 72V e-bike batteries?","a":"Higher voltage delivers power more efficiently to a compatible motor and controller, but the system must be designed for it. Never swap voltages without confirming the controller and motor support it. See the batteries, chargers & electronics range for current models and prices."},{"q":"How should I store an e-bike battery?","a":"Store it in a cool, dry place away from flammable items, partly charged if left for long periods. Do not leave it on charge unattended or store it in extreme heat. Browse the batteries, chargers & electronics category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What batteries and chargers does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The batteries, chargers and electronics category lists batteries, chargers, controllers and wiring harnesses for several bikes. Each page names the models it fits. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Should I buy a bigger battery or a second battery?","a":"A bigger battery adds range in one pack but adds weight. A second battery lets you swap and keep riding, and spreads wear. Choose based on how long you ride and whether your bike supports two packs. Browse the batteries, chargers & electronics category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list e-bike batteries and chargers?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists e-bike batteries and chargers in the batteries, chargers & electronics category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"e-bike batteries guide","supporting":["e-bike batteries guide","how to choose e-bike batteries","e-bike batteries explained","e-bike batteries for beginners","e-bike batteries tips"]},"words":649},"kids-electric-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"A kids’ electric dirt bike is a small battery-powered off-road bike with limited power. Match the bike to your child’s height, ability and the manufacturer’s age guidance.","intro":["A kids’ electric dirt bike is a small battery-powered off-road bike for young riders. It is quiet, easy to start and has no clutch, which many children find less intimidating than a petrol bike.","Choosing the right one depends on your child’s size and ability more than their age. These bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia."],"sections":[{"h":"Age, height and ability","p":["Age is a poor guide on its own. Check the rider height and weight range on the product page, and think about your child’s confidence and coordination. A bike that is too big or too powerful is unsafe."]},{"h":"Power limiter and speed settings","p":["Look for a bike with power modes or a speed limiter so you can start slowly and increase power as your child improves. Check that the brakes are easy for small hands to operate."]},{"h":"Safety gear","p":["A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and protective armour are essential every ride. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/) and browse [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]},{"h":"Balance bike or mini dirt bike first?","p":["An electric balance bike is a gentler first step for younger children learning balance. A mini dirt bike suits older, more confident riders. See the [electric balance bike guide](/blog/electric-balance-bikes/), and our [Yozma IN10 guide](/blog/yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/) for one popular mini bike."]},{"h":"Learning to ride: a simple progression","ul":["Start in an open, flat area on a low-power setting.","Practise starting, stopping and turning slowly.","Add gentle slopes and obstacles as confidence grows.","Increase the power setting only when your child is ready."]},{"h":"Supervision and riding rules","p":["Always supervise. Set clear rules about where to ride, staying away from other people and stopping when told. Check the bike before every ride, and keep the area free of vehicles and animals."]},{"h":"Maintenance and growing out of the bike","p":["Check brakes, tyres, chain and the battery regularly. Children grow quickly, so check the rider size on the bike and plan the next step. A well-kept bike can often be sold when your child outgrows it."]},{"h":"What to look for in a first bike for a child","ul":["A speed limiter or low-power mode.","A seat height the child can reach with both feet.","Brakes with levers sized for small hands.","A throttle that returns to idle when released.","A kill switch or lanyard."]},{"h":"Teaching safe riding habits","p":["Start with the basics: how to start, stop and fall, and how to stay away from others. Set boundaries on where the child can ride and practise emergency stops. Supervise every ride, and stop sessions before the child gets tired."]},{"h":"Sizing a bike for a child","p":["Seat height and weight matter more than age. A child should be able to sit on the bike with both feet reaching the ground, or at least the balls of their feet, and operate the brake levers comfortably. Check the maker’s rider height and weight guidance and, if possible, have your child sit on the bike before you buy.","Avoid buying a bigger bike for your child to grow into. A bike that is too large is harder to control and can lead to falls. Buy for the size now, and plan to move up when the child outgrows it."]},{"h":"Choosing protective gear for children","ul":["A helmet that fits snugly and meets the standard for the type of riding.","Goggles to protect against dust and branches.","Gloves, long sleeves and long pants.","Boots with ankle support.","Chest and knee protection for off-road riding."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**What age can a child ride?** It depends on size and ability. Check the maker’s guidance and supervise.","**Do kids need a licence?** On private property there is generally no road licence, but check venue and landowner rules.","**Is electric safer than petrol?** It is simpler to operate, but it can still cause injury. Gear and supervision matter.","**How long should a first ride be?** Short sessions of 10 to 15 minutes are plenty at first."]},{"h":"Where children can ride","p":["Use private property or an approved off-road area, with adult supervision. Check your state’s rules and the land owner’s conditions, and read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/). Browse the [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) category."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What age is a kids’ electric dirt bike suitable for?","a":"Suitable age depends on the model, your child’s height and ability, so follow the manufacturer’s guidance on each product page. Start with the smallest, lowest-power setting and supervise every ride. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do kids need protective gear on an electric dirt bike?","a":"Yes. A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and body armour reduce injury risk. Check that gear is sized for children and replace helmets after a hard impact. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which kids’ electric bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists mini dirt bikes and electric balance bikes from several brands, and the youth category lists youth e-bikes. Each page shows size and price. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Should I buy a balance bike or a mini dirt bike first?","a":"An electric balance bike is a gentler first step for younger children learning balance and control. A mini dirt bike suits older children with more experience. Match the bike to your child’s size and confidence. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list kids’ electric bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists kids’ electric bikes in the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"kids’ electric dirt bikes guide","supporting":["kids’ electric dirt bikes guide","how to choose kids’ electric dirt bikes","kids’ electric dirt bikes explained","kids’ electric dirt bikes for beginners","kids’ electric dirt bikes tips"]},"words":624},"electric-vs-petrol-dirt-bikes":{"hook":"Electric dirt bikes are quieter and need less maintenance than petrol bikes, but ride time is limited by the battery. Petrol bikes refuel in minutes and suit very long days.","intro":["Electric and petrol dirt bikes both ride off-road, but they feel and work differently. Electric bikes are quiet, simple and instant, while petrol bikes offer long ride times and quick refuelling.","This guide compares them on the points that matter when choosing, without pretending one is right for everyone."],"sections":[{"h":"Noise and where you can ride","p":["Electric bikes are far quieter, which can make it easier to find places to ride and keep neighbours happy. Whichever you choose, ride only on private property or approved off-road areas and follow local rules."]},{"h":"Maintenance","p":["Electric bikes have no engine oil, air filter, spark plug or clutch to maintain, so routine servicing is simpler. They still need brake, chain, tyre and battery care: see our [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/) and [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Power delivery and learning","p":["Electric motors deliver torque instantly and smoothly with no clutch or gear changes, which many beginners find easier. Instant power also needs respect, so start in a low mode and wear full protective gear."]},{"h":"Ride time and refuelling","p":["A petrol bike refuels in minutes, while an electric bike needs charging, which takes longer. Ride time on an electric bike depends on battery capacity and how hard you ride, so for long days consider a second battery or a bike with a larger pack."]},{"h":"Cost of ownership","p":["Electric bikes use electricity rather than fuel and need fewer routine services, but batteries are expensive and wear over time. Petrol bikes cost more to service and fuel but have cheaper replacement parts in many cases. Compare the whole running cost over a few years, not just the purchase price."]},{"h":"Power, weight and handling","p":["Electric bikes often feel lighter and more instant, while petrol bikes provide a familiar engine feel and engine braking. Weight distribution differs, with battery weight low and central on an electric bike. Try both if you can, because feel is personal."]},{"h":"Environment and neighbours","p":["Electric bikes produce no exhaust and little noise, which helps with neighbours and some riding areas. Whichever you choose, ride only where it is permitted and follow local rules."]},{"h":"Choosing based on how you ride","ul":["Short sessions near home: electric is quiet and convenient.","All-day riding far from power: petrol offers faster refuelling.","Learning or riding with kids: electric power delivery is easier to manage.","Racing: check which class and rules apply at your venue."]},{"h":"Environmental and neighbour considerations","p":["Electric bikes produce no exhaust and are much quieter, which helps with neighbours and some venues. Both types still need to be ridden only where allowed and with respect for others."]},{"h":"Practical differences you notice on the day","p":["Starting an electric bike is a matter of switching it on, while a petrol bike needs fuel, a kick or button start and sometimes a warm-up. An electric bike is almost silent, so you hear the tyres and the trail rather than an engine, which many riders enjoy. A petrol bike has a long-established parts and service network, while an electric bike needs fewer routine services.","On the trail, an electric bike’s torque is instant and smooth, which helps on technical climbs. A petrol bike’s engine braking and power band feel different, and many riders prefer them for long, fast days. Neither is better for everyone."]},{"h":"Questions to ask yourself before choosing","ul":["How long do I ride in a typical session?","Where will I ride, and is noise a problem there?","How much maintenance am I happy to do?","Who will ride the bike, and how experienced are they?","Where will I charge or refuel, and how do I transport the bike?"]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**Which is cheaper to run?** Electric bikes have lower running costs, but batteries wear over time.","**Which is quieter?** Electric bikes, by a wide margin.","**Which has more range?** Petrol bikes refuel quickly, and electric bikes depend on battery size.","**Which is better for kids?** Electric is simpler to operate, with no clutch or gears."]},{"h":"Which is better for beginners?","p":["Electric bikes are easier to start and have smoother, clutch-free power, which many beginners prefer. Browse [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) and read the [electric dirt bike guide](/blog/best-electric-dirt-bikes-in-australia/) to compare models."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Are electric dirt bikes quieter than petrol bikes?","a":"Yes. Electric motors are much quieter than petrol engines, which can make them easier to ride near neighbours and on land with noise limits. Check local rules about riding on your property. See the electric dirt bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Do electric dirt bikes need less maintenance?","a":"They have no engine oil, air filter or spark plugs to service, so routine maintenance is mostly chains, brakes, tyres and the battery. They still need regular checks and cleaning. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"What electric dirt bikes does Torque E-Bikes sell?","a":"The electric dirt bikes category lists Sur-Ron, Talaria, Segway, KTM, Husqvarna and more, with prices in Australian dollars. Kids’ models are in a separate category. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is electric or petrol better for beginners?","a":"Electric bikes are easier to start and have smoother, clutch-free power, which many beginners prefer. Petrol bikes teach clutch and gear use. Either way, start slowly and wear full protective gear. Browse the electric dirt bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric dirt bikes and the petrol comparison?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric dirt bikes and the petrol comparison in the electric dirt bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric vs petrol dirt bikes guide","supporting":["electric vs petrol dirt bikes guide","how to choose electric vs petrol dirt bikes","electric vs petrol dirt bikes explained","electric vs petrol dirt bikes for beginners","electric vs petrol dirt bikes tips"]},"words":631},"electric-balance-bikes":{"hook":"An electric balance bike is a pedal-free kids’ bike with a small motor. It helps children learn balance and throttle control with limited power.","intro":["An electric balance bike is a pedal-free bike with a small motor, designed to help young children learn balance and throttle control. Many parents see it as a stepping stone to a mini dirt bike or a bicycle.","This guide explains who it suits and what to check before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"How it differs from a pedal balance bike","p":["A pedal-free balance bike teaches balance very simply. An electric one adds a throttle, so the child also learns speed control. That makes supervision and protective gear more important."]},{"h":"Who it suits","ul":["Children who already balance well on a push bike.","Families riding on private property.","Children moving up towards mini dirt bikes."]},{"h":"What to check","ul":["Rider height and weight range.","A speed limiter or low-power mode.","Brakes that small hands can operate.","Battery run time and charging.","Tyre type and suspension."]},{"h":"Safety and where to ride","p":["Always supervise, use a fitted helmet and protective gear, and ride only on private property or an approved off-road area. Read the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Moving up to a pedal bike or mini dirt bike","p":["Once a child balances confidently, steers well and uses the brakes without thinking, they are ready to move on. A pedal bike builds fitness and road skills, while a mini dirt bike builds throttle control. Choose the next bike for the size and ability your child has now."]},{"h":"Care and charging","p":["Charge the battery with the supplied charger under adult supervision, and store the bike indoors at a moderate temperature. Check the tyres, brakes and bolts before each ride."]},{"h":"Safety gear for small riders","p":["A properly fitted helmet is essential, and gloves, knee and elbow pads add protection. Supervise every ride and ride on a safe surface away from traffic."]},{"h":"Choosing the right size","p":["Check the seat height and the child’s inside leg. They should be able to put both feet flat on the ground while seated. A bike that is too tall is unsafe, and one that is too small is uncomfortable. Many models have an adjustable seat, which helps as the child grows."]},{"h":"First rides and progression","ul":["Start at the lowest speed setting.","Practise on flat, soft ground.","Teach braking and stopping first.","Increase speed only when the child is confident."]},{"h":"How an electric balance bike teaches balance and throttle control","p":["A normal balance bike teaches a child to balance first, because they push along with their feet and lift them as they gain confidence. An electric balance bike adds a motor, so the child also learns to manage speed with a throttle. The sequence matters: balance first, then power. A child who can already glide on a push bike with their feet up will adapt to a motor quickly, while a child who still wobbles may be frightened by the first surge of power.","Look for a bike that lets a parent limit speed, ideally with a clearly marked low setting that keeps the bike at walking or jogging pace. Some bikes have a parent-controlled remote or a physical limiter. Whichever system you choose, test it yourself before your child rides, so you know how the bike responds to the throttle."]},{"h":"Safety rules for a first-time young rider","ul":["Choose a flat, open and safe area away from roads, driveways and water.","Fit a properly sized helmet every time, plus gloves and pads if the child will accept them.","Teach the child to release the throttle and stop before you teach them to go faster.","Keep rides short, because young children tire quickly and make mistakes when tired.","Check the bike before each ride: tyres, brakes, bolts and battery.","Stay close enough to reach the child, and agree on simple signals such as “stop” and “slow”."],"p":["The aim of the first few weeks is confidence, not distance. When your child is comfortable at low speed, you can move up a setting, but do it one step at a time, and go back a step if confidence drops."]},{"h":"Battery, charging and storage for a child’s bike","p":["Use the charger supplied with the bike, charge it where you can see it and unplug it when it is full. Do not let children charge the battery themselves. Store the bike indoors in a cool, dry place, and take the battery out if the manual recommends it. If the bike will not be used for a few weeks, leave the battery partly charged. See our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/) for the full routine."]},{"h":"Browse the range","p":["See the [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) category and [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How is an electric balance bike different from a normal one?","a":"An electric balance bike adds a small motor and throttle to the pedal-free frame, so a child learns balance and throttle control together. Look for a speed limiter and good brakes. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What size electric balance bike does my child need?","a":"Match the wheel size and seat height to your child’s inseam so their feet touch the ground. Check each product’s recommended height range, and choose a size they can grow into slightly. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which electric balance bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists electric balance bikes in several wheel sizes from multiple brands. Prices and size guidance are on each page. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Is an electric balance bike better than a pedal one?","a":"A pedal-free balance bike teaches balance more simply. An electric one adds throttle control and is suited to riders ready for a bit more speed. Choose by your child’s age and confidence. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Where does Torque E-Bikes list electric balance bikes?","a":"Torque E-Bikes lists electric balance bikes in the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category, with the specification and the price in Australian dollars on each product page. The team can confirm fitment, stock or freight on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 before you order."}],"keywords":{"primary":"electric balance bikes guide","supporting":["electric balance bikes guide","how to choose electric balance bikes","electric balance bikes explained","electric balance bikes for beginners","electric balance bikes tips"]},"words":692},"talaria-x3-mx3-and-mx4":{"hook":"Talaria makes a range of electric off-road bikes with different frames, motors and batteries. Check each model’s official specification, because details change between model years.","intro":["Talaria makes several families of electric off-road bikes, and model names such as X3, MX3 and MX4 refer to different bikes with different frames, motors and batteries. People search for these names a lot, so it helps to know how they fit together before you choose.","A note before we start: the Talaria X3, MX3 and MX4 are not currently listed at Torque E-Bikes. This guide explains how to compare them and points you to the Talaria models we do list, and you can ask the team what can be sourced."],"sections":[{"h":"How the Talaria range is organised","p":["Talaria sells model families under names such as X3, MX3, MX4, Sting and XXX. They differ in frame size, motor power, battery capacity and intended rider. Because specifications change between model years, use the manufacturer’s official page as the source for exact figures."]},{"h":"What to compare between models","ul":["Motor power and the riding modes available.","Battery capacity and charging time.","Weight, seat height and suspension travel.","The rider size and experience the model suits.","Spare parts and upgrades available in Australia."]},{"h":"Talaria models we list","p":["The Talaria models in our catalogue include Sting and XXX bikes. See the full list on the [Talaria brand page](/brands/talaria/) and in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category. For a wider comparison read the [Talaria range guide](/blog/talaria-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [Sur-Ron models guide](/blog/sur-ron-models-explained/)."]},{"h":"Off-road use and Australian rules","p":["Talaria bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia. They are not road legal. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and wear full protective gear: see the [gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"How to check a model before you buy anywhere","ul":["Confirm the exact model name and year on the maker’s page.","Compare motor, battery and weight with other models you are considering.","Ask what is included, such as the charger and tools.","Check warranty terms and how claims are handled in Australia.","Make sure parts are available locally."]},{"h":"Why model names cause confusion","p":["The same model can have different versions, colours and market-specific changes. Check that the version you are looking at matches the one reviewed or discussed online, and ask the seller to confirm the specification."]},{"h":"Choosing an alternative from the range","p":["If you cannot find the model you want, think about what you were drawn to: lightweight handling, power, price or brand. Then compare the Talaria Sting and XXX models and other bikes in the [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) category on those measures. The team can help narrow the choice."]},{"h":"What the model names usually tell you","p":["Model names such as X3, MX3 and MX4 are the maker’s way of separating its bikes by frame, power level and intended rider. They are not interchangeable, and the differences can be large. Names also change between model years, which is why reading the official specification matters more than relying on a nickname or a forum thread.","If you are comparing models, make a short table with motor power, battery capacity, weight, seat height, suspension and price. Fill it from the maker’s own information, and check each figure at least twice if it will decide the purchase."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller before you buy any Talaria","ul":["Is this the exact model and year I have been reading about?","What is included: charger, tools, spare parts and manual?","What is the warranty and how are claims handled in Australia?","Are parts and upgrades available locally?","What are the delivery times and freight costs for a bike with a lithium battery?"]},{"h":"Asking about a model that is not listed","p":["Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with the model and year you want. We will tell you what is available, the price in Australian dollars and the freight cost before you pay."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I compare between Talaria models?","a":"Compare motor power, battery capacity, weight, suspension and the rider size each model suits. Use the maker’s official specification for each model year, because details change, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you want help comparing two bikes. See the talaria range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can I ride a Talaria X3, MX3 or MX4 on the road?","a":"No. Electric dirt bikes and e-bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only, and are not road legal or registrable. Check your state or territory road authority and wear full protective gear whenever you ride. Browse the talaria category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How is the Talaria range organised?","a":"Talaria sells several model families with different frames and power levels, and names such as X3, MX3, MX4, Sting and XXX refer to different bikes. Check the official page for each model before comparing prices or parts. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the talaria range."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes list the Talaria X3, MX3 or MX4?","a":"The Talaria hub lists the Talaria models currently in the catalogue, including Sting and XXX models, and the X3, MX3 and MX4 are not among them at the time of writing. If the model you want is not listed, message the team and they will tell you what can be sourced and when."},{"q":"Can I ask Torque E-Bikes about a Talaria model that is not listed?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 or use the contact form with the model and year. They will confirm what is available, the price in Australian dollars and the freight cost before you pay. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"talaria x3","supporting":["talaria x3 pro","talaria mx4","talaria mx3","talaria x3 australia","talaria 3x"]},"words":554},"yozma-in10-electric-mini-dirt-bike":{"hook":"The Yozma IN10 is an electric mini dirt bike for young riders. Check the rider height and age guidance, wear full protective gear and ride only on private property or approved off-road areas.","intro":["The Yozma IN10 is an electric mini dirt bike for young riders. It is quiet and easy to start, and it suits children who are ready to move up from a balance bike or a smaller bike.","This guide explains who it suits, how to set it up and how to ride it safely. It is for off-road and private property use only."],"sections":[{"h":"What the Yozma IN10 is","p":["The Yozma IN10 Electric Mini Dirt Bike is listed in our [kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) and [electric dirt bikes](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/) range. Open the [Yozma IN10 product page](/shop/electric-dirt-bikes/yozma-in-10-electric-mini-dirt-bike/) for its specification and price in Australian dollars."]},{"h":"Who it suits","p":["Suitability depends on your child’s height, weight and ability, not age alone. Check the rider size on the product page, and the manufacturer’s manual. A child should be able to reach the ground and operate the brakes and throttle comfortably."]},{"h":"Setup, charging and first ride","ul":["Read the manual and check every bolt and fitting before the first ride.","Charge the battery with the supplied charger, in a ventilated spot, and never leave it charging unattended.","Start in a low-power mode in an open, flat area.","Check brakes, tyres and chain before each ride."]},{"h":"Protective gear","p":["A fitted helmet, goggles, gloves, boots and armour are essential every ride. See the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/) and browse [helmets, gloves and protection](/shop/helmets-gloves-and-protection/)."]},{"h":"Safety checks before every ride","ul":["Check tyres for pressure and damage.","Test both brakes before moving.","Check the chain and bolts.","Check the battery is secure and charged.","Make sure your child is wearing full gear."]},{"h":"Growing with the bike","p":["Rider size is the limit, not age. When your child reaches the maximum height or weight, move up to a larger bike. See the [kids’ electric dirt bikes](/shop/kids-electric-dirt-and-balance-bikes/) range."]},{"h":"Choosing protective gear for a young rider","ul":["A helmet that fits snugly and meets the standard.","Goggles for dust and sun.","Gloves, boots and long sleeves.","Knee and elbow guards and a chest protector."]},{"h":"Questions to ask before ordering","ul":["What is the maximum rider weight and height?","Does it have speed modes?","What is the battery run time?","What does the warranty cover?"]},{"h":"Understanding the Yozma IN10 as a first off-road bike","p":["Electric mini dirt bikes like the Yozma IN10 are designed to make off-road riding less intimidating for young riders. There is no clutch to stall, no fuel to mix and no loud engine to startle a nervous child. The controls are simple: a throttle, a brake and, on some models, a speed setting. That simplicity helps children focus on balance, steering and body position.","The bike is still a motorised vehicle, and a young rider will push its limits quickly. Start in a low-power setting, keep sessions short and make sure the child understands that the throttle must be released to slow down. If the bike has a lanyard or kill switch, use it so the motor stops if the child falls off."]},{"h":"Choosing where and how to ride","ul":["A fenced yard, a paddock or a motocross track with permission from the owner.","Flat, firm ground with no steep slopes or deep ruts for the first sessions.","No traffic, animals or people in the riding area.","A clear boundary the child understands, and an adult who can see the whole area."],"p":["Do not ride on public roads, footpaths or in parks. The bike is for private property and approved off-road areas only. Our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) explains why."]},{"h":"Looking after the bike between rides","p":["Wipe dirt off the frame and wheels, check the tyres and chain, and look for loose bolts. Keep the battery dry and charge it after riding. Replace worn brake pads and tyres early, because a child will not tell you when the brakes are getting weak. Store the bike out of the sun and away from moisture."]},{"h":"Where to ride","p":["Use private property or an approved off-road area with adult supervision. Read the [kids’ electric dirt bike guide](/blog/kids-electric-dirt-bikes/) and the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) for where and how children can ride."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What age is the Yozma IN10 suitable for?","a":"Suitability depends on the child’s height, ability and the manufacturer’s guidance rather than age alone. Check the rider size on the product page, supervise every ride, and fit a helmet and protective gear before the first ride. See the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can the Yozma IN10 be ridden on the road?","a":"No. Electric mini dirt bikes are for off-road and private property use only in Australia, and they are not road legal or registrable. Use a fenced yard, a private property or an approved off-road area. Browse the kids electric dirt & balance bikes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How should I charge and store the Yozma IN10 battery?","a":"Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and never leave it charging unattended. Store the bike somewhere cool and dry, away from flammable items, and follow the manufacturer’s manual for battery care. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the kids electric dirt & balance bikes range."},{"q":"Does Torque E-Bikes list the Yozma IN10?","a":"Yes. The Yozma IN10 Electric Mini Dirt Bike is listed with its price in Australian dollars on its product page, and the team can confirm stock and freight on WhatsApp before you order. Dirt bikes are for off-road use only."},{"q":"Where can I see other kids’ electric bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The kids’ electric dirt and balance bikes category lists mini dirt bikes and electric balance bikes, and the kids and youth e-bikes category lists larger models. Each product page shows the specification and price. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"yozma in10","supporting":["yozma in 10 australia","yozma in10 electric bike","yozma in 10 electric bike","yozma in 10 pro electric dirt bike","yozma in10 guide"]},"words":610},"dirodi-rover-gen-6":{"hook":"The DiroDi Rover is a fat-tyre electric bike. Check the motor, battery and rider limits on the product page, and confirm that the bike meets the rules where you plan to ride.","intro":["The DiroDi Rover is a fat-tyre electric bike, and the Gen 6 is one of the versions people search for most. Wide tyres give grip on sand, gravel and rough tracks, and the motor and battery make longer rides easier.","This guide explains what to check before you buy, how range works and where the Rover fits in the fat-tyre range."],"sections":[{"h":"What to check on a DiroDi Rover","ul":["Motor power and whether it is pedal assist.","Battery capacity, which decides range.","Weight and the rider limit.","Brakes, tyres and frame size."],"p":["See the [DiroDi Rover Gen 6 product page](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/dirodi-rover-gen6/) if it is available, and the [DiroDi brand page](/brands/dirodi/) for every DiroDi model we list, including the Rover Pro."]},{"h":"Range on a fat-tyre bike","p":["Range depends on battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, tyre pressure and assist level, so treat the maker’s figure as a best case. Soft sand and hills reduce it, and a larger battery adds range and weight. Our [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/) explains how tyre pressure affects it."]},{"h":"Rules for where you can ride","p":["Rules depend on the bike’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while more powerful bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Check the product page and read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]},{"h":"Accessories and upgrades","p":["Lights, a rack and a good lock make a Rover more useful for commuting and touring. See the [lights guide](/blog/e-bike-lights/), the [racks guide](/blog/bike-racks-baskets-and-trailers/) and the [lock guide](/blog/how-to-lock-an-e-bike/)."]},{"h":"Setting up a new bike","ul":["Read the manual and check every bolt.","Set tyre pressure for your surface.","Adjust the saddle and handlebars to fit you.","Charge the battery fully before the first ride.","Test the brakes in a safe area."]},{"h":"Maintenance for wide-tyre e-bikes","p":["Check tyre pressure often, clean the drivetrain after sandy or muddy rides, and check brake pads regularly. Rinse salt from the bike after beach riding, avoiding high-pressure water on electrical parts."]},{"h":"Choosing between Rover models","p":["The Rover range includes several versions with different power and battery options. Compare motor power, battery capacity and weight on the product pages, and check which version suits your riding and the rules in your area. The Rover Pro and Rover Plus Gen 6 are listed alongside the Gen 6 on the [DiroDi brand page](/brands/dirodi/)."]},{"h":"Who the DiroDi Rover suits","p":["Fat-tyre e-bikes like the Rover suit riders who want stability and comfort on rough or soft surfaces, and who value a relaxed ride over a light, sporty feel. The wide tyres soak up bumps and give grip on gravel, sand and dirt tracks, and the motor makes up for the extra rolling resistance. They are popular for beach paths, fire trails, farm tracks and general leisure riding.","They are heavier than ordinary e-bikes and take more room to store and transport. If you need to lift the bike onto a rack or carry it up steps, check the weight on the product page and think about whether you can handle it. A removable battery can reduce the weight when lifting."]},{"h":"Things to check when the bike arrives","ul":["Look for transport damage on the frame, wheels, display and battery.","Check that bolts, pedals, handlebars and brakes are tight.","Set tyre pressure for your surface and weight.","Charge the battery fully before the first ride, using the supplied charger.","Test the brakes in a safe area before you ride."]},{"h":"Maintenance for sandy and wet conditions","p":["Sand and salt are hard on drivetrains and bearings. After riding on a beach or in wet conditions, rinse the frame gently with fresh water (avoid direct high-pressure spray on the motor, battery and display), dry the bike and lubricate the chain. Check the brake pads and rotors for grit, and store the bike somewhere dry."]},{"h":"Other fat-tyre e-bikes","p":["Compare the Rover with other bikes in the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, and read the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What should I check before buying a DiroDi Rover Gen 6?","a":"Check the motor power, battery capacity, weight and rider limit on the product page, then match them to where you will ride. Ask the team on WhatsApp if you want the specification explained before you order. See the dirodi range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Is the DiroDi Rover Gen 6 legal to ride on roads?","a":"Rules depend on the bike’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist e-bikes that meet the 250W standard can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are for off-road and private property use only. Check your state road authority. Browse the dirodi category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How far can a fat-tyre e-bike go on one charge?","a":"Range depends on battery capacity, rider weight, terrain, tyre pressure and assist level, so treat the maker’s figure as a best case. Soft sand and hills reduce it, and a larger battery adds range and weight. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the dirodi range."},{"q":"Which DiroDi bikes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The DiroDi hub lists the DiroDi Rover models in the catalogue, including the Rover Gen 6 and Rover Pro. Each product page shows the specification and the price in Australian dollars, and freight is confirmed before payment. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where can I see other fat-tyre e-bikes at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes category lists wide-tyre bikes from several brands. Open a product page for specifications and price, and message the team on WhatsApp if you want two models compared. Open the dirodi category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"dirodi gen 6","supporting":["dirodi rover v6","dirodi rover plus gen 6","dirodi rover gen 3","dirodi rover pro 1000w","dirodi rover gen 5"]},"words":592},"flight-risk-fr505-and-fr555":{"hook":"Flight Risk makes fat-tyre electric bikes in several models. Compare the motor, battery and frame on each product page, and check which models are listed before you decide.","intro":["Flight Risk makes fat-tyre electric bikes in several models, and the FR505 and FR555 are two of the names people search for. They are different bikes, so compare their specifications rather than relying on the name.","Torque E-Bikes lists the FR505, FR777 and FR888. The FR555 is not currently listed. This guide explains how to compare them and what to check."],"sections":[{"h":"The Flight Risk range","p":["Flight Risk sells a range of fat-tyre bikes with different motors, batteries and frames. See the models we list on the [Flight Risk brand page](/brands/flight-risk/), and the [Flight Risk FR505 product page](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/flight-risk-fr505/)."]},{"h":"Comparing the FR505 and FR555","p":["Compare motor power, battery capacity, weight, brakes and frame on the maker’s specification for each model year. Use the table on the maker’s page as the source, and be cautious about forum figures."],"ul":["Motor and drive type.","Battery capacity and range.","Weight and rider limit.","Brakes and suspension.","Frame size."]},{"h":"What to look for in a fat-tyre e-bike","p":["Check the tyre width, motor, battery, brakes and frame size, and think about the surface you ride on. Wide tyres suit sand, gravel and rough tracks but add weight, so match the bike to how you ride. Our [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/) covers this in more detail."]},{"h":"Rules and safe riding","p":["It depends on the model’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are off-road only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/), wear a helmet, and see the [protective gear guide](/blog/helmets-and-protective-gear-for-e-bikes/)."]},{"h":"Questions to ask the seller","ul":["What are the exact motor power and battery capacity?","What is the model year and what is included?","What does the warranty cover?","Are spare parts available in Australia?","Is the bike pedal assist, throttle or both?"]},{"h":"Charging and care","p":["Use the supplied charger, charge in a ventilated spot and do not leave the battery charging unattended. Check the tyres, brakes and bolts regularly, and store the bike dry. See the [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/)."]},{"h":"Deciding between the Flight Risk models listed","p":["Torque E-Bikes lists the FR505, FR777 and FR888. Compare their specifications on the product pages, think about weight and range, and choose the one that suits how and where you ride. If you want a model that is not listed, ask the team what can be sourced."]},{"h":"Understanding what separates models in a fat-tyre range","p":["Within a single brand’s fat-tyre range, the differences usually come down to motor power, battery size, frame design and component quality. A model with a bigger battery will go further but weigh more. A model with a stronger motor will climb better but may be heavier and more expensive. Suspension, brakes and drivetrain components also vary, and affect both comfort and reliability.","Rather than choosing by model number, decide what matters to you most: range, climbing, weight, comfort or price. Then look at the specifications of each model and see which suits those priorities. Where a number is missing, ask the seller."]},{"h":"Getting the most from a fat-tyre e-bike","ul":["Set tyre pressure for the surface: lower for sand and snow, higher for hard roads.","Use a lower assist level on flat ground to extend range.","Keep the battery between about 20% and 80% for daily use if the maker recommends it.","Carry a pump and spare tube that suit the wide tyres.","Check bolts and brakes regularly, because heavier bikes stress components."]},{"h":"Rules and where to ride","p":["Check the motor’s power rating and whether the bike has a throttle. Pedal-assist bikes within the 250W limit can be used on roads and paths, subject to state rules, and more powerful bikes are for private property only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/) and ask the team if you are unsure about a particular model."]},{"h":"Where to see them","p":["Browse the [fat-tyre and scrambler e-bikes](/shop/fat-tyre-and-scrambler-e-bikes/) category, or message the team on WhatsApp to ask about a model that is not listed."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What is the difference between the Flight Risk FR505 and FR555?","a":"They are different models with different specifications, so compare motor power, battery capacity, weight and frame on the maker’s page for each model year. Do not rely on the name alone, and ask the team on WhatsApp for help comparing."},{"q":"Are Flight Risk e-bikes road legal in Australia?","a":"It depends on the model’s motor power and your state. Pedal-assist bikes at 250W or less can use public roads, while higher-powered bikes are off-road only. Check the product page and your state road authority before riding. See the flight risk range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What should I look for in a fat-tyre e-bike?","a":"Check the tyre width, motor, battery, brakes and frame size, and think about the surface you ride on. Wide tyres suit sand, gravel and rough tracks but add weight, so match the bike to how you ride. Browse the flight risk category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Which Flight Risk models does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The Flight Risk hub lists the models currently in the catalogue, including the FR505, FR777 and FR888. If you want a model that is not listed, message the team and they will check availability. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp."},{"q":"Where can I find the Flight Risk FR505 at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The Flight Risk FR505 has its own product page with the specification and the price in Australian dollars. You can also reach it through the Flight Risk hub or the fat-tyre e-bikes category. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"flight risk 505","supporting":["flight risk 555","flight risk fr555","flight risk 777","flight risk 505 guide","how to choose flight risk 505"]},"words":589},"e-bike-tyres-and-tubes":{"hook":"The size and valve type are printed on the tyre sidewall. Match them when you buy a replacement tyre or tube, and replace tyres that are cracked, worn flat or repeatedly puncturing.","intro":["Tyres are the only thing connecting your bike to the ground, and e-bike tyres carry extra weight and torque. Matching the size and valve type, and keeping the pressure right, improves safety, grip and range.","This guide covers how to read tyre sizes, choose tubes, set pressure and spot when to replace them."],"sections":[{"h":"How to read an e-bike tyre size","p":["The size is printed on the sidewall, for example 26 x 4.0 or 700 x 40c. The first number is the approximate diameter and the second is the width. You may also see an ETRTO number, such as 40-622, which gives width and bead diameter. Match both the diameter and the width when you replace a tyre."]},{"h":"Tubes and valve types","p":["Tubes have either a Schrader valve (like a car tyre) or a Presta valve (thinner, with a lock nut). Your rim has a hole drilled for one of them, so use the matching valve type, and choose a tube sized for your tyre width. Tubeless tyres use sealant instead of a tube and need tubeless-ready rims."]},{"h":"Tyre pressure","p":["Use the pressure range printed on the tyre sidewall and adjust within it for load and surface. Lower pressure grips better on rough ground, while higher pressure rolls easier on smooth roads and usually improves battery range. Check pressure before each ride with a gauge."]},{"h":"Tyre types for e-bikes","ul":["Commuter tyres with puncture protection.","Road and gravel tyres for sealed and unsealed surfaces.","Knobbly tyres for off-road riding.","Fat tyres for sand and soft ground."]},{"h":"When to replace a tyre","p":["Replace a tyre when the tread is worn flat, the sidewall is cracked or cut, or it punctures repeatedly. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so worn tyres reduce braking grip and safety."]},{"h":"Fixing a puncture","ul":["Remove the wheel and tyre, and find the cause of the puncture.","Fit a new tube or patch the old one.","Check inside the tyre for thorns or glass.","Refit the tyre without pinching the tube and inflate to the right pressure.","On a hub-motor wheel, take care with the cable and torque the axle nuts to the maker’s setting."]},{"h":"Tubeless and puncture protection","p":["Tubeless tyres use sealant to seal small punctures and can run at lower pressures. They need compatible rims and tyres. Puncture-protection belts and thicker tubes help commuter bikes avoid flats."]},{"h":"Choosing tyres for your riding","ul":["Smooth, puncture-protected tyres for commuting.","Semi-slick tyres for mixed road and path riding.","Knobbly tyres for trails and loose ground.","Wide fat tyres for sand and snow."]},{"h":"Storing and caring for tyres","p":["Keep tyres out of direct sun and away from oil. Check them for cuts and embedded glass after each ride, and replace tubes that have been patched many times. Keep a spare tube and a pump with you."]},{"h":"Tubeless, tubes and puncture protection","p":["Most e-bikes use tubes, which are simple and cheap to replace. Tubeless tyres use sealant instead and can run at lower pressures with fewer flats, but they need tubeless-ready rims and tyres and a little more setup. Puncture-protection layers inside commuter tyres add resistance to thorns and glass, and are worth having if you ride in town.","Whatever you choose, check the tyre’s load rating, because e-bikes are heavy. A tyre rated for a lighter bike may not suit an e-bike with a rider, battery and luggage."]},{"h":"A tyre check routine","ul":["Check pressure weekly, and before long rides.","Look for cuts, bulges and embedded objects.","Check tread depth and wear patterns.","Spin the wheel and look for wobble or flat spots.","Replace tyres in pairs if both are worn."]},{"h":"Carrying a repair kit","p":["A spare tube, tyre levers, a pump or CO2 inflator and a multi-tool will get you out of most puncture problems. Learn how to remove your wheel before you need to, especially if it has a hub motor, because the cable and torque arm need care. See the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category."]},{"h":"Quick answers","ul":["**How do I know my tyre size?** Read the sidewall.","**Can I fit wider tyres?** Only if the frame, fork and rim allow it.","**How often should I check pressure?** Weekly, and before long rides.","**Should I go tubeless?** It can reduce flats, but needs compatible rims and tyres."]},{"h":"Products and related guides","p":["See the [wheels, tyres and tubes](/shop/wheels-tyres-and-tubes/) category and the [care, tools and hydration](/shop/care-tools-and-hydration/) category for puncture repair gear. Read the [fat-tyre e-bike guide](/blog/fat-tyre-e-bikes/) for wide-tyre bikes."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I read the size on an e-bike tyre?","a":"The size is printed on the sidewall, for example 26 x 4.0 or 700 x 40c. Match both the diameter and the width when you replace a tyre, and check that the tube valve suits the rim. See the wheels, tyres & tubes range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What tyre pressure should I use on an e-bike?","a":"Use the range printed on the tyre sidewall and adjust within it for load and surface. Lower pressure grips better on rough ground, while higher pressure rolls easier on smooth roads and usually improves battery range. Browse the wheels, tyres & tubes category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"When should I replace an e-bike tyre?","a":"Replace a tyre when the tread is worn flat, the sidewall is cracked or cut, or it punctures repeatedly. E-bikes are heavier and faster than normal bikes, so worn tyres reduce braking grip and safety. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the wheels, tyres & tubes range."},{"q":"What tyres and tubes does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The wheels, tyres and tubes category lists tyres, tubes and related wheel parts. Each product page states the size and the bikes it suits, and the team can confirm fitment on WhatsApp before you order. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where do I find tools for fixing a puncture?","a":"The care, tools and hydration category lists tools and care products, and the wheels, tyres and tubes category lists tubes. Message the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure what suits your wheel size. Open the wheels, tyres & tubes category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"bike tyre pressure","supporting":["cycle tyre pressure","bike tire psi","psi for cycle tyres","tubeless bike tyres","how to air up a bike tire"]},"words":680},"e-bike-seats-saddles-kickstands-and-mudguards":{"hook":"Seats, mudguards and kickstands are fitted to specific mounts. Check the mounting type and the model before ordering, and tighten every bolt before riding.","intro":["Seats, mudguards and kickstands are fitted to specific mounts on your bike, so the right size and mounting matter more than the look. Choosing well improves comfort and keeps the bike tidy and practical.","This guide covers how to choose and fit each part."],"sections":[{"h":"Choosing a seat or saddle","p":["Comfort comes from width, shape and padding that suit your body and riding. A wider, padded saddle suits upright commuting. A narrower saddle suits sportier riding. Check the rail type so it fits your seat post clamp."]},{"h":"Seat posts and clamps","p":["Measure the outside diameter of the seat tube or post with callipers, or read the size marked on the post. Common sizes include 27.2 mm, 30.9 mm and 31.6 mm. A post that is too small can slip, and one that is too large will not fit the frame."]},{"h":"Kickstands","p":["Check the mount type on the frame, such as a plate by the rear axle or a dedicated bolt, and choose a stand of the right length. Tighten it fully and check that it supports the bike’s weight, because e-bikes are heavy."]},{"h":"Mudguards and fenders","p":["Match the mudguard to the wheel diameter and the tyre width, and leave clearance for the tyre to turn. Fat tyres need wide mudguards, and some frames need specific mounting points."]},{"h":"Setting saddle height and position","p":["Set the saddle so your leg is nearly straight at the bottom of the pedal stroke, with a slight bend in the knee. Adjust the fore-aft position and tilt for comfort, and check the clamp is tight. A saddle that is too high or too low causes knee and back pain."]},{"h":"Seat covers and body parts","p":["Seat covers protect a seat from weather and add colour, and body parts such as fenders and panels fit specific models. Check the compatibility on the product page."]},{"h":"Getting a comfortable fit","ul":["Set the saddle height so your knee has a slight bend at the bottom of the stroke.","Level the saddle, then adjust the tilt a few degrees at a time.","Move the saddle forward or back to suit your reach.","Choose a saddle width that suits your sit bones."]},{"h":"Mudguards, kickstands and practical fittings","p":["Mudguards keep you clean and dry, and a kickstand rated for the bike’s weight makes loading easier. Check that these fittings do not clash with tyres, brakes or racks."]},{"h":"Saddle shape and comfort","p":["A saddle that is comfortable for one rider can be painful for another, because sit-bone width and riding position differ. Wider, padded saddles suit upright riding, and narrower saddles suit more forward positions. Cut-outs or channels can reduce pressure. If you ride long distances, pay more attention to fit than to padding, because too much padding can cause chafing.","Give a new saddle a few rides before judging it, and adjust height, angle and position in small steps. A saddle that is tilted too far up or down can cause numbness."]},{"h":"Mudguards and fittings that last","ul":["Choose mudguards with sturdy stays and enough clearance for your tyres.","Check that fittings are rated for the weight of an e-bike.","Use thread locker on bolts that rattle loose.","Replace cracked mudguards, because they can jam in the wheel."]},{"h":"Kickstands and bike stands","p":["A good kickstand matters on a heavy bike. Check the rated load, the length and the mount type, and test it with the bike loaded. A kickstand that is too short or too long makes the bike lean or fall. A centre stand or a twin-leg stand is more stable for cargo bikes."]},{"h":"Footrests, pegs and body parts","p":["Foot pegs and body parts fit specific models, so check the compatibility on the product page. See the [seats, fenders and body](/shop/seats-fenders-and-body/) category, and ask the team on WhatsApp if you are unsure about fit. For the whole commuting setup read the [commuter e-bike guide](/blog/commuter-e-bikes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"How do I measure a seat post for a replacement?","a":"Measure the outside diameter of the seat tube or post with callipers, or read the size marked on the post. A post that is too small can slip, and one that is too large will not fit the frame. See the seats, fenders & body range for current models and prices."},{"q":"What size mudguard do I need?","a":"Match the mudguard to the wheel diameter and the tyre width, and leave clearance for the tyre to turn. Fat tyres need wide mudguards, and some frames need specific mounting points. Browse the seats, fenders & body category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"How do I fit a kickstand on an e-bike?","a":"Check the mount type on the frame, such as a plate by the rear axle or a dedicated bolt, and choose a stand of the right length. Tighten it fully and check it supports the bike’s weight. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the seats, fenders & body range."},{"q":"Which seats, kickstands and mudguards does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The seats, fenders and body category lists seats, seat covers, kickstands, foot pegs and body parts for several bikes. Each product page names the models it fits. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications. Open the seats, fenders & body category to see everything that is currently in stock."},{"q":"Can Torque E-Bikes confirm whether a seat fits my bike?","a":"Yes. Message your bike’s make, model and year on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 and the team will check fitment before you order. Freight and price are confirmed before payment. The team can confirm fitment or availability on WhatsApp. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."}],"keywords":{"primary":"kickstand on a bike","supporting":["bicycle saddle post","saddle tube bike","cycle saddle clamp","kickstand on mtb","bicycle front fender"]},"words":589},"e-bike-grips-pedals-and-handlebar-controls":{"hook":"Grips, pedals and handlebar controls are the contact points on an e-bike. Match the grip diameter, pedal thread and controller connector to your bike before you order.","intro":["Grips, pedals and handlebar controls are the contact points between you and your e-bike. The right sizes and connectors make the bike comfortable and safe, and the wrong ones cause problems.","This guide covers how to choose and fit them."],"sections":[{"h":"Choosing grips","p":["Most flat-bar bikes use 22.2 mm (7/8 inch) handlebars, but throttle and display fittings can change the space available. Measure your bar, check the grip length, and check that a twist throttle fits alongside the grip. Lock-on grips stay put without glue, and slip-on grips are simple but can rotate."]},{"h":"Pedals","p":["Flat pedals suit casual and off-road riding, and clipless pedals suit sporty riding with matching shoes. Pedal threads differ left and right: the left pedal has a reverse thread and the right pedal a normal one, usually marked L and R. Most e-bike pedals use a 9/16 inch thread, but check your cranks first."]},{"h":"Brake levers and handlebar controls","p":["Many e-bikes use brake levers with a cut-off sensor that stops motor power when you brake. Check that a replacement lever has the right connector for your controller before you fit it, and that the lever suits your brake type."]},{"h":"Fitting tips","ul":["Tighten to the maker’s torque settings.","Use thread-locking compound where recommended.","Check that controls do not clash with each other or the display.","Test the brakes and throttle before riding."]},{"h":"Comfort, ergonomics and vibration","p":["Ergonomic grips with a wider contact area reduce hand fatigue on long rides, and padded grips cut vibration. Set brake levers at a comfortable reach and angle so your wrists stay straight."]},{"h":"Pedal grip and shoes","p":["Pedals with pins give grip with flat shoes, and clipless pedals hold your shoe to the pedal for efficiency. Match pedals to your shoes and riding, and practise releasing clipless pedals before riding in traffic."]},{"h":"Choosing pedals for your riding","ul":["Flat pedals with pins for grip and easy dismounts.","Platform pedals with reflectors for commuting.","Clipless pedals for sporty riding with matching shoes."]},{"h":"Fitting grips and controls properly","p":["Clean the bar, slide the grip on with a little grip adhesive or use lock-on clamps, and tighten to the maker’s settings. Set brake levers so you can reach them with one or two fingers and your wrist straight. Test brake and throttle operation before you ride."]},{"h":"Choosing grips for comfort and control","p":["Grips are a small part with a big effect on comfort. Thicker grips and ergonomic shapes spread pressure across your hand and reduce numbness on long rides. Softer rubber absorbs vibration, while firmer grips give a more direct feel. If your bike has a twist throttle, the grip next to it needs to be compatible, and if you ride in gloves, consider grips with a textured surface.","Lock-on grips use clamps and do not need adhesive, so they are easy to remove and adjust. Slip-on grips are cheaper but can rotate if they are not fitted well. Whichever you choose, tighten clamps to the maker’s setting and check them after the first ride."]},{"h":"Pedals, cranks and thread problems","ul":["Check which way each pedal threads on: the left pedal is reverse threaded.","Grease the threads and tighten firmly to avoid creaking and damage to the crank.","Do not force a pedal that will not start, because you can strip the crank.","Replace worn pedals, because loose or damaged pedals can cause accidents."]},{"h":"Controls and displays","p":["Check that displays and switches suit your controller before buying. Many e-bikes use proprietary connectors, so a part that looks the same may not fit. If you are unsure, send the team your bike’s make and model and ask them to check before you order. See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category."]},{"h":"Products and related guides","p":["See the [controls, grips and pedals](/shop/controls-grips-and-pedals/) category and the [brakes](/shop/brakes/) category. Read the [pedal assist vs throttle guide](/blog/pedal-assist-vs-throttle/) and the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"What size are e-bike handlebar grips?","a":"Most grips fit a 22mm handlebar, but throttle and display fittings can change the space available. Measure your bar and check the grip length before you order, and check that a twist throttle fits the grip. See the controls, grips & pedals range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Are bicycle pedals left and right threaded?","a":"Yes. The left pedal has a reverse thread and the right pedal has a normal thread, and they are usually marked L and R. Most e-bike pedals use a 9/16 inch thread, but check your cranks first. Browse the controls, grips & pedals category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Do brake levers on an e-bike cut the motor?","a":"Many e-bikes use brake levers with a cut-off sensor that stops motor power when you brake. Check that a replacement lever has the right connector for your controller before you fit it. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the controls, grips & pedals range."},{"q":"What grips and pedals does Torque E-Bikes list?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists handlebar grips, pedals, throttles, displays and switches. Each product page states the models it fits and the connector type where it matters. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Where do I find a replacement display or throttle?","a":"The controls, grips and pedals category lists displays and throttle kits for several bikes. Message your bike’s make and model on WhatsApp and the team will confirm which one is compatible. Open the controls, grips & pedals category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"pedal of a bike","supporting":["cycle grip","pedal on a bike","bikes with pedals","pedal of a bike guide","how to choose pedal of a bike"]},"words":591},"conversion-kit-finder":{"hook":"A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle. The right kit depends on the bike type, rider weight, terrain and whether you will ride on roads or off-road only.","intro":["A conversion kit adds a motor, battery and controller to a bicycle. The right kit depends on your bike type, your weight, the terrain and whether you will ride on roads or off-road only.","Use the finder below to narrow the choice, then check the fitment notes on the product pages before you buy."],"sections":[{"h":"How the finder works","p":["Choose your bike type, rider weight, terrain and where you will ride. The finder suggests a kit type from our range and gives a plain-English legal note. It is a guide, not a guarantee of fit, so confirm compatibility with the team."]},{"h":"What else you will need","p":["A conversion also needs a compatible battery, good brakes and installation time. Read our [conversion kit guide](/blog/e-bike-conversion-kits-explained/) and our [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/)."]},{"h":"Common questions the finder helps answer","ul":["Do I need a hub motor or a mid-drive?","How much power do I really need?","How big should the battery be?","Is my frame strong enough?","Can I use this on the road?"]},{"h":"After the finder: next steps","p":["Use the suggestion to shortlist kits, then check each product page for fitment notes and specifications. Measure your dropout width, wheel size and brake type, and check them against the kit. If you want a second opinion, send the team your bike’s make, model and photos of the frame and dropouts."]},{"h":"Rules for converted bikes","p":["A pedal-assist conversion that stays within the 250W limits can result in a road-legal e-bike. Higher-powered kits are for off-road and private property use only. Read the [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/)."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Which conversion kit do I need for my bike?","a":"It depends on the bike type, rider weight, terrain and whether you will ride on roads. Answer the four finder questions and compare the result with the fitment notes on the product pages before you buy. See the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range for current models and prices."},{"q":"Can any bicycle be converted to an e-bike?","a":"Not every frame suits a kit. Check the dropout width, brake type, wheel size and frame strength, and think about the extra weight of the battery. A bike shop can confirm whether your frame is suitable. Browse the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to compare models side by side."},{"q":"Is a converted e-bike road legal in Australia?","a":"Only pedal-assist conversions that meet the 250W standard can be used on public roads, subject to state rules. Kits above 250W are for off-road and private property use only. Check your state road authority first. Message the team on WhatsApp if you want advice on the motors, drivetrain & sprockets range."},{"q":"Which conversion kits can the finder point me to at Torque E-Bikes?","a":"The motors, drivetrain and sprockets category lists conversion kits, motors and drivetrain parts. Each product page shows the specification and the price in Australian dollars, and the team confirms fitment on request. Prices are in Australian dollars and each product page lists its specifications."},{"q":"Can the team help me choose a conversion kit?","a":"Yes. Message the team on WhatsApp on 0480 804 189 with your bike’s make, model and wheel size and where you ride. They will suggest kits from the range and confirm freight before you pay. Open the motors, drivetrain & sprockets category to see everything that is currently in stock."}],"keywords":{"primary":"ebike conversion kit finder","supporting":["which conversion kit do i need","ebike conversion kit compatibility","conversion kit for my bike","conversion kit size guide","what ebike conversion kit fits my bike"]},"words":232},"diy-2000w-conversion-guide":{"hook":"A 2000W conversion is an off-road machine for private property use only in Australia. Plan the frame, torque arms, battery, wiring and brakes before you buy parts.","intro":["A 2000W conversion turns a mountain bike into a very powerful off-road machine. It is not a road-legal e-bike: bikes above 250W are for off-road and private property use only in Australia.","This guide is a planning checklist rather than a step-by-step build, because the right steps depend on your parts. It focuses on the decisions that most often go wrong: frame strength, torque arms, wiring, battery safety and brakes. If you are not confident with high-current electrical work, have a qualified person do it."],"sections":[{"h":"Check the frame first","p":["Standard mountain bike frames are not designed to withstand high motor torque. The reactive force on the dropouts can bend or crack an aluminium frame, so a strong steel or purpose-built frame is safer, and torque arms are essential. If you are unsure of your frame’s strength, choose a lower-powered kit."]},{"h":"Torque arms and wheel build","p":["Fit heavy-duty torque arms on both sides of the axle so the motor cannot spin in the dropouts. Check that the wheel is built with strong spokes and rim for the extra load, and that the axle nuts are torqued to the maker’s settings."]},{"h":"Battery, wiring and safety","ul":["Use a quality battery with a protection board, and the charger supplied with it.","Use wire gauge rated for the current, and secure connectors so they cannot arc or loosen.","Fit a fuse or breaker suited to the system.","Keep the battery out of heat, and never charge it unattended."],"p":["Read our [battery care guide](/blog/electric-moto-maintenance-lithium-safety/). Lithium batteries are Class 9 dangerous goods, so shipping needs approved carriers."]},{"h":"Brakes and tyres","p":["A bike that goes faster and weighs more needs better brakes and tyres. Upgrade to strong hydraulic disc brakes and check rotor size, and make sure tyres suit the speed and load. See the [brakes guide](/blog/e-bike-and-dirt-bike-brakes/) and the [tyre guide](/blog/e-bike-tyres-and-tubes/)."]},{"h":"Chain and drivetrain","p":["If the kit uses the bike’s chain, align and tension it carefully. See the [chain care guide](/blog/chain-alignment-high-torque-motors/)."]},{"h":"Test before you ride","ul":["Check every bolt, torque arm and connector.","Lift the wheel and test the motor with the wheel off the ground.","Test the brakes and brake cut-off.","Ride slowly in a safe, private area first."]},{"h":"When to ask for help","p":["If you are unsure about wiring, frame strength or brakes, ask a qualified person to check the build before you ride it. A conversion is only as safe as its weakest part."]},{"h":"Charging and testing safely","p":["Charge the battery in a safe place, check the pack and connectors for damage and test the system at low power first. Never leave a high-power battery charging unattended, and keep a suitable fire extinguisher nearby."]},{"h":"Parts list for a high-power build","ul":["A motor and controller rated for the power you intend.","A battery with the right voltage, capacity and current rating, and a protection system.","Torque arms for both sides of the axle.","Heavy-duty spokes and a strong rim.","Brakes suited to the extra speed and weight.","Wire and connectors rated for the current, plus a fuse or breaker.","A display or controller panel and a throttle.","Tools: torque wrench, crimper, multimeter, thread locker and heat-shrink."]},{"h":"Common reasons builds fail","ul":["Frame or dropout failure from too much torque.","Overheating connectors from undersized wire or poor crimps.","Brake failure from underrated brakes.","Battery damage from an unsuitable charger or poor storage.","Wheel failure from weak spokes or a weak rim."]},{"h":"Rules and gear","p":["A 2000W build is for private property use only. Read our [e-bike laws guide](/blog/off-road-vs-street-legal-australia/), wear full protective gear, and see the [conversion kit finder](/tools/conversion-kit-finder/) to match a kit to your bike."]}],"faq":[],"keywords":{"primary":"diy 2000w conversion","supporting":[]},"words":551}}
